WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:02.693
- Welcome.

00:00:03.886 --> 00:00:05.880
It's so exciting to see you all here.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.680
It's great to see everybody
and have everybody.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.990
Thank you so much for
coming out on this cold day.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.640
I'm Stephen Pitti,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.800
professor of History
American Studies in ERM

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.170
and Director of Yale Center
for the Study of Race,

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.780
Indigene Transnational Migration,

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.480
which has helped to support this event.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.420
Really happy to remind you at the outset

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.510
that we in New Haven here
are sitting on territory

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:31.410
that's long been the home of
Mohegan, Mashantucket Pequot,

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.530
Eastern Pequot, Schaghticoke,
Golden Hill Paugussett,

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.890
Niantic, Quinnipiac and other
Algonquian speaking peoples,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.062
and that we continue
ourselves to be revitalized

00:00:41.062 --> 00:00:44.160
by their survivance every day.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.960
We're delighted to help support this event

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.960
as our ITM, along with Ezra
Stiles College at Yale.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.030
And I want to thank a number of people

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.430
for making this happen,

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:59.370
Kathy Dunn and Mark
Levinson in Ezra Stiles,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.130
Nicole Edwards and Maggie
Katz and Mary Taylor Lewis

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.280
and Victoria Stone Kana in our ITM

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.180
and the Andrew W Mellon Foundation
for providing the funding

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.680
that made this event possible.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.940
Let me also recognize
that we have today with us

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.980
the Poet Laureate of the
state of Connecticut,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.590
Antoinette Brim-Bell, who's here

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.080
and who has joined us today.
(audience clapping)

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.930
Thank you Antoinette, for being here.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.480
I've also asked before we turn the podium,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.310
or so-called, over to
our guests here in front

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:35.520
to just say a few logistical words.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.860
First, I wanna make sure everybody knows

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:42.000
that the Yale University Art
Gallery closes at 5:00 PM

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.730
so if that, if you left any
belongings in the lockers

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.280
in the art gallery, you're gonna
need to retrieve those now,

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.630
or at least before five o'clock,

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:49.980
'cause the gallery will be closed.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.570
You won't be able to get
back into the lockers

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.900
for your keys, your wallets,
your jackets, and all the rest.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.930
So please do go fetch things
from the lockers before 05:00.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:58.763
There's really no way

00:01:58.763 --> 00:02:01.770
to open up the building after that time.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:05.850
Second, although I'm not
demonstrating it now,

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:10.380
the Art Gallery requires
the use of face masks

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.937
in this space so please,
please remain masked

00:02:13.937 --> 00:02:16.263
throughout the event while you're here.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:21.780
That's very important to our
speaker and to all of us.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.880
Third, wanted just to alert everybody

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.700
that this event is being recorded.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.170
You'll see there's a camera in the back

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.550
and there may be other cameras,

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.850
so just wanted to make sure
that you're aware of that.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.900
Finally, logistically, we're
gonna open the program up

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.260
for audience questions in
the last roughly 20 minutes

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.500
of our event today, so
please, when that time comes,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.180
if you do have a question,
use the microphone,

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.920
which I think will be circulating.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.250
So that our guest can hear
you and so that everybody else

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:51.960
in the room can hear you as well.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.230
I'll add that at the very, very end

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.900
we'll have time for audience
members to come forward

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.360
to request a book signing by Ada Limón

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.400
at the end of the event,
around six o'clock.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.490
It's gonna be pretty
short, we have a hard stop.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.070
It'll be a pretty quick
session, but we'll do our best

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:12.818
to accommodate as many people
as want to have a book signed.

00:03:12.818 --> 00:03:17.160
I'm gonna get outta the way,
but as I leave the stage,

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.500
I have the pleasure of
introducing the real host

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.605
of this event, Professor
Alicia Schmidt Camacho,

00:03:22.605 --> 00:03:25.688
(audience clapping)

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.170
whom I know quite well.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.510
At Yale, Professor Schmidt Camacho

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.760
is the head of Ezra Stiles College.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.100
She's a faculty member in and former chair

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.010
of the Program in Ethnicity,
Race and Migration

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:44.850
and she's a scholar whose
work has focused on migration,

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.520
the US Mexico border, Latinx studies,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.710
and other top topics and fields.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.200
I'll avoid staying too
long to sing her praises,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.910
but I'll just invite you
to join me in thanking

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:55.803
and welcoming Alicia Schmidt Camacho.

00:03:56.916 --> 00:03:59.999
(audience clapping)

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:10.500
- Okay, this is really
beautiful. (giggles)

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.320
I have the joyful task of introducing

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.710
the 24th Poet Laureate, Ada Limón,

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:20.040
and opening our
conversation this afternoon.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.683
What a gift it is to be together.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.210
Thank you, Ada Limón, for
giving us this occasion

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:30.783
to gathering community
in the service of poetry.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.730
Ada Limón, as you know, grew
up in Sonoma, California

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:37.920
and resides in Lexington, Kentucky,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.990
where she writes and teaches.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:42.180
She's the author of six books of poetry,

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.430
including "The Carrying," which won

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.853
the National Books Critics
Circle Award for poetry.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:54.210
She hosted the Celebrated
Poetry podcast, The Slowdown

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:58.620
and her new book of poetry
"The Hurting Kind" is out now

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.710
from Milkweed Editions and is a candidate

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.953
for the 2023 Southern Book Prize.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.880
And I wanna thank my colleagues

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.337
at the Yale Center for the
Study of Race, Indigeneity

00:05:11.337 --> 00:05:15.570
and Transnational
Migration for the students

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:18.810
and colleagues in Ezra
Stiles and around campus and

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.220
in Ethnicity, Race and Migration

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:24.180
and other communities who've
shared the love of poetry

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.523
with me and shared so much with me.

00:05:27.484 --> 00:05:30.750
And I wanna give special
thanks also to our host,

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:34.140
the Yale University Art
Gallery for welcoming us

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.950
and I wanna thank someone
else who's not here,

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:41.640
Vaughn Fielder,
representative for Ada Limón,

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:45.180
who runs something
called The Field Office,

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.450
where she makes the most
exquisite books and broadsides.

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:49.713
Please look for it.

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:55.230
Before we begin, I wanna mention
how marvelous it has been

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.033
to prepare for this occasion.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:03.110
Ada, your arrival has helped
give us a chance to gather

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.603
and be in celebration,
something we needed.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.570
We in Ezra Stiles,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:13.320
we've began talking about
inviting Ada Limón some time ago.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:16.230
And since we announced the visit,

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:20.160
I keep hearing testimony
from so many people about all

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.160
that her poetry and her work
has represented for them.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.830
And in the week between
drafting my message of inquiry

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.080
and actually sending it,

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.393
Ada Limón was named the Poet Laureate.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.383
But you were our Laureate first.

00:06:36.383 --> 00:06:38.550
(audience laughing)

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.130
And I don't remember many moments

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.250
where I actually could trust
my government's judgment,

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.740
(audience laughing)

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:51.513
least of all, in anything as
important as poetry and art.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.390
But the Office of Poet Laureate

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:56.850
housed in the Library of Congress

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.190
may be the one authority in the land

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.498
to whom I will happily submit.

00:07:01.498 --> 00:07:04.080
(audience laughing)

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.250
And it was beautiful to watch
the Librarian of Congress,

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:11.340
Carla Hayden, name you to this post Ada,

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.940
to talk about your poetry
being a force in the world.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:19.350
One that fosters connection,
intimacy, healing,

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:22.230
one that invites us to the table.

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:26.220
And Tracy K. Smith, also a Poet Laureate,

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.820
called you a poet of ecstatic revelation,

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:34.170
whose poems offer deep wisdom
and urgent vulnerability,

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:37.500
driven by language that
feels not only beautiful

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.553
but permanent and powerfully
wrought like a mountain.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:46.170
That is certainly how we've
experienced your work here.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:49.560
Your poems have been integral
to our life as a community.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.890
They've given us a language
for finding one another,

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:55.830
for coming together in difficult moments,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:08:00.830
for imagining living beyond
the time of pandemic,

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:03.980
for imagining how we can thrive.

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.013
Thank you.

00:08:06.865 --> 00:08:08.820
And so I'm gonna begin,

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.460
move over here so that we
can begin more conversation

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.328
and Ada can face out to see all of you.

00:08:14.328 --> 00:08:17.328
(audience clapping)

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:26.363
- Thank you.

00:08:27.341 --> 00:08:28.740
- Can you hear me?

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.702
Okay. Oh, yes you can.

00:08:30.702 --> 00:08:33.510
(both laughing)

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.960
So my favorite moment,

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.393
when you were at the Library of Congress,

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:46.050
was right at the beginning
after you'd been announced

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:48.510
and you stepped up and
you greeted everyone.

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:51.960
You sort of announced
your arrival in this post

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:56.960
and you said, "We roll deep." (laughing)

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:03.090
We roll deep.
(Ada giggles)

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.440
And it was a moment of recognition for me

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:10.110
because I think again, like
what are we trying to do here?

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.507
We're trying to gather ourselves together

00:09:12.507 --> 00:09:16.773
to build power together
in something precious.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.370
Precious, the way that art is.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:23.070
Precious, the way that
art a accompanies us

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:24.633
in our lives when we need it.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:27.780
How we be together.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.130
And so it struck me that we roll deep

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.310
was kind of an artist statement

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:39.230
as well as a mission for you
in this role of Poet Laureate

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:42.630
and I wanted to ask you about that

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:47.630
because in your art you
contain multitudes, you know?

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.153
Your art brings multitudes.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:57.150
- I love that. Thank you so much for that.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.490
I feel like it's-

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:01.770
First of all, beautiful introduction.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:05.070
And it's just such a gift
to be here with you all.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:06.633
Thank you for coming out.

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:09.930
And I think one of the things

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.260
that I've always sort of, pushed against

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:16.173
is that sort of myth
that we make art alone.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.523
And that that myth that
like one person rises,

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:23.793
I just don't believe it.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.023
I have always believed that
we make art in community,

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.910
even if what we're working on
aren't exactly collaborations.

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:35.950
What we're actually doing
is often reaching out

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:36.783
to one another and saying,

00:10:36.783 --> 00:10:38.190
"Hey, will you read this one poem?"

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:40.470
Or "Hey, will you listen to this song?"

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:42.777
Or "Hey, will you come to this play?"

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:47.610
You're always reaching out
and you're always getting

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.313
that support from the people you need.

00:10:51.783 --> 00:10:54.854
And that to me is all part
of the art making process.

00:10:54.854 --> 00:10:56.400
And so when I'm saying we roll deep,

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:59.840
I really do believe that we do that

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:03.570
even if deep means two people, right?

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:06.780
I'm not saying you have
to have this huge network

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:08.520
of people to support you,

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:11.700
sometimes you need one
other person, you know?

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.517
That just says, "Yeah, yeah,
yeah, this, keep doing it."

00:11:15.501 --> 00:11:17.070
And sometimes they don't
even need to critique it.

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.590
Sometimes it's better if
they don't. (laughing)

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:22.470
Sometimes it's just better if
they say "Yes, keep going."

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:24.780
But I think it's important to remember

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:29.780
that sometimes it can look
like one person is doing a lot,

00:11:29.862 --> 00:11:33.510
in reality, a lot of people
are doing a lot of things

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.283
and all of us are in community.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:37.943
- Thank you.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.150
And Ada, as I told you earlier today,

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:44.670
at the onset of the pandemic,

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:48.780
we really had to reinvent
the university, you know,

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:52.350
for the conditions of
separation and isolation.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.390
And that's really hard.

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:59.390
And one thing that arose, I
think in many parts of the world

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:04.323
were these chain mail messages.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:10.220
And I was lucky to be
receiving messages with poetry

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:12.517
and then the instruction would be,

00:12:12.517 --> 00:12:16.140
"Here's this poem, now
you have to send your poem

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:17.760
to these 10 other strangers."

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.450
And it went around

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:24.310
and it was international and

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:29.220
eventually, you know, I was
getting repeats from people,

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:33.273
so it was clear that this was
doing some great work, right?

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:35.850
And your poems were there.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:36.990
- Oh.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:37.980
- More often than not.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:41.040
The one that I always put
out there was "Dead Stars".

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:44.070
That one really works on me.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:48.660
But then when I took up my
post as the Head of College

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:53.010
and was writing a weekly
newsletter, my dean,

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:54.120
Dean Temples here with me,

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:57.480
we were trying to figure
out how to build connections

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.930
to students and to build
a sense of community

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:05.460
across many time zones for
students who were in spaces

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:09.900
where, maybe learning was
the last thing on their mind

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.600
during the especially difficult days.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:14.070
How do we be present for them?

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:15.423
How do we invite them in?

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:19.860
And how do we have the
conversations about things

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:22.773
that we really need to talk about, right?

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:26.590
And again, your poems gave me a way

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:31.560
to give a piece of something beautiful,

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.360
something to think about,

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:36.453
but most often, really invite people in.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.140
And I put a lot of
different poems out there

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:43.860
and I did notice that the ones from you,

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:45.750
I kept hearing more.

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:50.250
And then also when students
were sending me ideas for poems

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:53.430
that they wanted to hear, they
were sending me your poems.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:54.507
- Oh, I love that.

00:13:54.507 --> 00:13:56.710
- And I've been thinking a lot about that

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:59.130
because there's a lot

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:02.280
of really very beautiful
poetry out there, right?

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:05.190
But there was something about,

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:09.450
your attention to the world,

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:12.513
your attention to emotion,

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:16.830
that really, I felt
like, was what we needed

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:18.690
in the university at that moment.

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:21.780
The university sometimes presents itself

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:25.800
as a space of learning where
we leave emotions aside

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:30.540
to achieve or to accomplish.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:33.363
And we receive wisdom but,

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:36.870
in your poetry, there's the knowledge

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:41.160
that we're really struggling
sometimes, you know?

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:45.960
That we're kind of a mess
and that we might hurt.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:50.250
And that that's actually
the best we can be right now

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:51.990
is true to what we're feeling.

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:55.830
And it felt to me like the
students were able to commune

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:59.280
with you and then through you, me,

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:02.460
because you made that space
and poetry made that space.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:05.730
And I wanted to ask you to maybe think

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:08.160
about the way you think about emotions

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:11.280
and also that I know you're a teacher,

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:14.403
how you might think about
the teaching in your poetry.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:16.200
- Yeah, thank you.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.550
That was really beautiful.

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:20.583
I think that one of the big
things for me is that there's,

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.950
people will say things like,
"Oh, poetry can do this."

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.687
Or "Poetry can do that."

00:15:27.687 --> 00:15:31.620
Or "You should read a poem and
it'll make you feel hopeful."

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:33.067
And we were laughing today, it's like,

00:15:33.067 --> 00:15:35.149
"Well that depends on what poem you read."

00:15:35.149 --> 00:15:36.480
(audience laughing)

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:37.500
You know?

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.510
Because if you read Philip Larkin's poem

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:42.690
about the groundhog, I don't
think you're gonna feel

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:44.493
very hopeful after that poem.

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:49.650
And so I think that there's a
lot of, sort of misconceptions

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:50.607
about what poetry can do.

00:15:50.607 --> 00:15:54.030
And I think it's only because
it limits it, you know?

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:55.140
And for me,

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:58.920
I think it's not just that
it makes you feel one thing

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:00.690
or another, it makes you feel happy,

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:01.950
it makes you feel hopeful,

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:05.610
makes you reconnect with
some sort of, you know,

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:08.310
long lost feeling that you have to dig up

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:11.700
from someplace you've been suppressing it.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.103
I think it's really that
it just makes you feel,

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:18.870
and I think that is important.

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:19.895
And I think that we've
been living at a time

00:16:19.895 --> 00:16:24.895
where we've just had to
numb so much of our lives

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:27.360
and a lot of that is necessary.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:29.760
Like I don't even think
that's necessarily a bad thing

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.730
that we've had to numb things
because it's really hard

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.080
to feel all the things
that we've been feeling.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:40.170
And I think poetry is that way
of sort of tapping into that

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:42.930
and being able to like,
"Oh, right, I am a thinking,

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:46.500
feeling human being going
through all of these things.

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.830
I have these complex
emotions working within me."

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:53.490
And in doing so, feeling
like, "Oh, I'm reminded

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:56.370
that this is what being
a human being is like.

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:58.590
It's not just putting my head down

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:01.290
and getting from point
A to point B," you know?

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:03.270
It's not just survival,

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:06.300
can it also be flourishing?

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:10.410
You know, can it also be
witnessing, experiencing?

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:15.300
And I think that's a big part
of what I've been trying to do

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.940
with my work for a long
time, which is like,

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:20.973
can it help me each time I write a poem,

00:17:22.076 --> 00:17:24.790
recommit in some way to the world

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:28.957
that each time I go to
the page, I'm saying,

00:17:28.957 --> 00:17:31.710
"Here I am, I'm here."

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:32.543
You know?

00:17:34.613 --> 00:17:37.800
It's like a map that says, "You are here."

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:39.627
Each poem is like, "You are here."

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:45.620
And that I think, feels just
as important as something

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.570
that might, perhaps, contain more answers

00:17:48.570 --> 00:17:50.460
or wisdom or knowledge.

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:54.150
Poetry doesn't do that.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:55.900
It contains more questions

00:17:57.060 --> 00:17:59.520
and the questions lead to more questions.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:02.460
And in that it's just
sort of accepting that

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:04.050
that fluidness of life

00:18:04.050 --> 00:18:08.310
and that this amorphous
aspect of changing and growing

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:11.553
and being more in tune with ourselves.

00:18:14.138 --> 00:18:16.359
You know, the great
poet, Sadie Wright said,

00:18:16.359 --> 00:18:18.930
"The goal is not to make a story

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:21.870
but to experience the whole mess."

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:24.120
And I think about that all the time

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:27.690
because we kind of wanna do
this tidy thing in our head,

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:29.400
this is what happened.

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:31.110
But really, you know,

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:33.843
our jobs are just to
experience the whole mess.

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.610
- And something pretty
extraordinary happens

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:44.970
in the way you guide
us to that mess, right?

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:46.950
And you hold us there.

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:49.620
It seems to me that it's no accident

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:53.790
that the volume before "The Hurting Kind"

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:55.380
was "The Carrying", right?

00:18:55.380 --> 00:19:00.380
There's so much required of us

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:02.220
to be carrying

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:05.370
and yet that caring is also
the richness of life, right?

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:08.280
And that's our vehicle for
joy and fullness, right?

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:10.833
That's the capacity to do that.

00:19:12.168 --> 00:19:16.320
And I just, you know, like many people,

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:20.460
I went to funerals in the last few years.

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:23.343
We've seen a lot of violence
in the last few years.

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:27.910
Intimate violence as well as
terrifying terror in the world

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.280
and one of the things
that I noticed is that,

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:37.110
it's like a saying now to
say, "There are no words"

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:39.780
when you're greeting someone in grief,

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:42.390
but of course there are, you know?

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:43.410
And that always seemed to me like,

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:47.130
why are we letting ourselves
off the hook, right?

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:49.957
How do we be present in these moments?

00:19:49.957 --> 00:19:53.760
How do we actually create
the language we need?

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:57.210
So again, I turn your poetry

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:02.210
because I think you're attentive
to where fear atomizes us

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:04.053
and pushes away from each other.

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:11.280
And you're not afraid to look
for language for the unsayable

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:14.580
to gesture towards what is
unsayable and to leave it there

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:15.990
for us to disentangle.

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:18.960
And so I'm gonna give
you an example of a line

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:21.693
that lives with me now.

00:20:22.916 --> 00:20:25.380
It's part of my mental
and spiritual furniture,

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:27.367
again, from "Dead Stars,"

00:20:27.367 --> 00:20:31.590
"I am a hearth of spiders these days.

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:33.867
A nest of trying."

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:37.653
There's so much happening there. Mm.

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:43.380
And earlier today when we
were talking with students,

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:44.940
which is beautiful,

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:49.320
you used the word ongoingness as a thing,

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:54.210
as something that is both
what we are tasked to do,

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:55.350
but is also glorious.

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:57.960
What a gift to experience
our own ongoingness!

00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:00.573
So again, I just wanna
invite you to talk about,

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:05.970
you're tapping into something for us there

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:09.270
and it's a lesson, but it's a lesson that,

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:12.300
it seems hard won, so I wanted to ask you

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:14.400
how you've thought about that?

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:17.490
- Yeah, thank you for that.

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:18.520
I feel like

00:21:23.250 --> 00:21:27.640
there's so much grief and
processing that we have to do

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:31.350
to just live. (laughing)

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:32.430
To just live.

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:33.592
And it's funny 'cause we can think

00:21:33.592 --> 00:21:37.770
that we don't have to grieve, right?

00:21:37.770 --> 00:21:39.667
And then like six months
later you're like,

00:21:39.667 --> 00:21:42.120
"Ugh, what's happening?"

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:45.870
You're like, "Oh right,
I am grieving this thing

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:48.570
that I didn't go through," you know?

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.193
Or, "That I thought I was fine"

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:54.007
and I think that that
keeps happening to us.

00:21:54.007 --> 00:21:55.650
And there's not just the
grief of the pandemic,

00:21:55.650 --> 00:22:00.570
but there's the species
loss and the plant loss

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:05.490
and like the planet itself
grieving from our own harm

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:07.530
and all of these things
that we're processing.

00:22:07.530 --> 00:22:11.820
And I think it's like anyone else,

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:15.093
it's very easy for me to
shut down to it, you know?

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:19.410
I'd much rather have a drink
and binge watch Netflix

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:23.670
than think about all the
wounds I wanna carry, you know?

00:22:23.670 --> 00:22:25.080
But at the same time,

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:29.040
I know that if I'm not working
through some of that stuff,

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:31.770
not all of it, I can't take it all on,

00:22:31.770 --> 00:22:34.383
but maybe a little part of
it, a little edge of it.

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:37.110
And if I can kind of tease it out

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:38.700
and work through it a little bit,

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:43.700
I know that I will be saner
and I know that I can be kinder

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:48.870
to other people 'cause It's my
way of being kind to myself.

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:51.483
And so in a lot of ways I think about,

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:57.000
there's this great Buddhist
monk named Lama Rod Owens.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:58.533
I don't know if you know him.

00:23:01.817 --> 00:23:04.347
He talks about that the goal is longevity.

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:06.720
And I think about that a lot

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:08.287
because it's very easy to think like,

00:23:08.287 --> 00:23:10.167
"I just need to get through the day,"

00:23:11.190 --> 00:23:14.430
or "I just need to get to
5:00" or whatever it is,

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:16.530
or "To get to hand this final in,"

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:17.880
or "To get this project done

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:20.487
and then I can start to
think about other things."

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:23.190
And he talks about longevity

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:26.010
because the way that we burn out, right?

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:29.400
The way that we burn out as
artists or whoever we are,

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:34.400
is by not dealing with some
of these big ticket topics

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:37.920
and just trying to sort of suppress them

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:40.590
or trying to take them all on at once.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:42.840
And then wondering why in a year from now,

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:45.060
we can't write anything, you know?

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:46.683
So I keep thinking about,

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:50.950
a lot of my poems are also
a way of care taking myself

00:23:52.976 --> 00:23:57.780
and a way of recognizing that my need

00:23:57.780 --> 00:24:02.520
to be alive and witnessing the world

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:05.100
is a need that I would like to continue

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:08.370
and so sometimes when
I'm going into the well,

00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:10.800
I have to give myself that ladder out.

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:12.041
Like I have to be like,

00:24:12.041 --> 00:24:17.041
"Okay, you've spent some
time down here, good for you.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:19.353
Now, where are the stairs?

00:24:20.508 --> 00:24:21.341
You know?

00:24:21.341 --> 00:24:23.160
And I think that's
really important for us,

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:25.230
not only as artists, but as human beings.

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:28.470
It's like, it's one thing
to face the darkness,

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:33.470
but it's another thing to
know that you have a way out.

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:40.440
And I think that that's very
much a part of me making poems,

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.450
that it's not just about,

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:44.880
I love when they land with someone

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:46.200
and how they land with someone

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:47.940
and hearing how it's affected.

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:50.010
It's like it's deeply emotional to me

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:52.417
and a little overwhelming
sometimes to hear someone say,

00:24:52.417 --> 00:24:54.753
"Oh, this moved me," I love that.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:57.930
But oftentimes when I'm making the poem,

00:24:57.930 --> 00:24:59.490
I'm not thinking about that.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:03.153
I'm thinking of, "How do I
get really honest with myself"

00:25:03.990 --> 00:25:08.340
and "How do I say something
that feels unsayable?"

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:11.790
And then, "How do I create
spaces within the poem

00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:14.943
where I can breathe a
little bit," you know?

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:20.940
And some of those poems
turn towards the light

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:22.200
and some of them turn away

00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:25.960
but hopefully there's enough
balance in them as they go

00:25:27.459 --> 00:25:30.053
that it feels like there's
a sense of wholeness there.

00:25:31.770 --> 00:25:33.720
- You've used a word a couple times

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:36.090
in this conversation, "witnessing."

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:36.990
- Yeah.

00:25:36.990 --> 00:25:40.260
- And it is there in your early poems,

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:45.260
but it is sort of a structuring
idea in "The Hurting Kind".

00:25:46.224 --> 00:25:47.848
- Mm hmm.

00:25:47.848 --> 00:25:48.681
- And,

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:55.580
I think of it as something
that you have developed

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:56.753
as a,

00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:02.680
vocation in this work that you do

00:26:03.773 --> 00:26:08.070
and I was thinking about how
you think about that term

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:12.240
because there's so much
in "The Hurting Kind"

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:14.940
about being a witness

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:19.020
and not needing the
things you're looking at

00:26:19.020 --> 00:26:20.280
to give you anything back.

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:22.773
In fact, the fox is
completely indifferent,

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:25.620
the birds are indifferent, right?

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:28.770
And, but the task of witness is a constant

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:32.800
and it's a process that
is revelatory for you

00:26:34.871 --> 00:26:36.570
and for us by reading.

00:26:36.570 --> 00:26:39.836
And the witnessing is so often directed

00:26:39.836 --> 00:26:42.870
toward the non-human world

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:47.160
and is often a way of affiliating
us with a non-human world.

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:49.923
And you've just spoken about that a bit.

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:52.560
And then I thought there's something else

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:55.020
that's really powerful about
your concept of witness

00:26:55.020 --> 00:26:59.687
that I think we forget, we
think witnesses is us seeing,

00:27:01.590 --> 00:27:04.770
attending and speaking to something,

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:08.070
but you really want us to
be there with our bodies.

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:11.360
You're attentive to the
body is a witness, right?

00:27:12.698 --> 00:27:14.277
You know, the body is to be present

00:27:14.277 --> 00:27:17.040
and the body experiences the
world and we have to make space

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:18.813
for how the body is in the world.

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:22.320
And there's a the beautiful
moment where the body is fragile

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:25.198
and you say, "The body is so body" and I-

00:27:25.198 --> 00:27:27.290
- (laughing) It is, so body.

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:29.310
- As a person who lives in a body

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:31.248
that is a bit puzzling sometimes.

00:27:31.248 --> 00:27:32.081
- Yeah.

00:27:32.081 --> 00:27:34.017
- I was like, "Oh, it's so body.

00:27:34.017 --> 00:27:35.460
- Yeah.

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.770
- But I just wanted to ask
you to talk a little bit

00:27:37.770 --> 00:27:40.191
about witness and what that means for you.

00:27:40.191 --> 00:27:41.160
- Yeah.

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:43.530
I love that question and the
many different layers to it.

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:47.160
I feel like there's, you know,

00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:52.160
to start with the body that
sounds like a Creeley poem.

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:55.440
He has that line right?

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:59.370
About the body, "The plan is
the body, the plan is the body"

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:01.110
he just says that over and over again.

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:02.133
It's wonderful.

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:07.880
I think that there's a level in which I,

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:13.593
because I have an undergraduate
degree in theater,

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:16.920
I was taught about the body as a tool

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:18.483
and the body as instrument.

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:23.940
And so when I moved to poetry,
breath work and you know,

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:28.940
sort of the "isness" of
the body was in the poems,

00:28:30.210 --> 00:28:32.640
because that's how you make things

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:36.330
was to encourage the body's presence

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.580
to be present within the body.

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:42.150
But I too, and I love that
word you said, "puzzling"

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:44.850
a body that can sometimes be puzzling.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:48.300
Who doesn't have a body that
can sometimes be puzzling?

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:51.510
Especially at a time where
they ask us about symptoms,

00:28:51.510 --> 00:28:54.000
it's like, "I think
I've had every symptom."

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:55.420
My friend Kristen Dombeck,

00:28:55.420 --> 00:28:58.050
who's a wonderful non-fiction writer,

00:28:58.050 --> 00:29:00.870
said she just wants to write
a book called Symptoms,

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:05.870
which is just all the symptoms
that all of her women friends

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:09.540
send her just on a daily basis.

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:12.236
Like, "So what are the symptoms today?"

00:29:12.236 --> 00:29:15.600
And so I think that I live
in a body that's, you know,

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:16.770
oftentimes in pain.

00:29:16.770 --> 00:29:19.890
I have, I try not to say
the word chronic pain

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:22.443
because I believe it will someday go away.

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:28.350
But I do live in a body
that's a little more difficult

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:30.693
to get around sometimes than others.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:35.070
And I experience vertigo
and I have scoliosis.

00:29:35.070 --> 00:29:37.380
So there's these, just
things, that kind of keep me

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:41.530
from being completely,
totally free in the body

00:29:42.509 --> 00:29:43.587
and so I think when I write my poems

00:29:43.587 --> 00:29:45.000
and I think about witnessing,

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:47.910
I'm thinking about how are all the senses

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:50.280
and I can't witness without being like,

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:53.670
well if I'm witnessing and
I'm taking a deep breath,

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:56.010
you think, "Oh my back hurts a little bit

00:29:56.010 --> 00:29:57.840
when I take a deep breath," right?

00:29:57.840 --> 00:29:59.880
There's all these other
things that are in the body

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:01.530
when I do that.

00:30:01.530 --> 00:30:03.630
I used to love it when,

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:05.433
meditation teachers would say,

00:30:06.847 --> 00:30:08.370
"Just sit and be in the body."

00:30:08.370 --> 00:30:09.337
And I'd be like,

00:30:09.337 --> 00:30:11.501
"Well, what if the body is the problem?

00:30:11.501 --> 00:30:13.748
(audience laughing)

00:30:13.748 --> 00:30:14.581
You know?

00:30:14.581 --> 00:30:15.420
I was like the one person on Zoom.

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:17.677
I was like, when the one
person on Zoom that I was like,

00:30:17.677 --> 00:30:19.980
"Oh, finally, I just get to be a head.

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:22.404
I've always only wanted to be a head."

00:30:22.404 --> 00:30:24.199
(audience laughing)

00:30:24.199 --> 00:30:25.767
I was like, "This is amazing!"

00:30:27.390 --> 00:30:29.577
Now it's like, "Okay body, we can do it."

00:30:30.450 --> 00:30:31.950
And so I think that that witnessing

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:35.490
had to include the blood,
the body, every part of it.

00:30:35.490 --> 00:30:37.080
And there's this funny when you talk

00:30:37.080 --> 00:30:39.240
about that moment about it,

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:42.630
because I remember in one of the poems

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:44.040
when I was writing "The Hurting Kind",

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:45.183
having this moment,

00:30:46.772 --> 00:30:50.850
it was during when we
were all really isolating

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:52.200
and we weren't, see I wasn't even,

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:57.200
it was just waving to neighbors
and feeling very alone.

00:30:57.300 --> 00:31:01.920
And I just literally crawled
on my belly onto the grass

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:05.190
and just like laid
there and got really low

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:08.970
and I kept thinking this
is like low snake energy.

00:31:08.970 --> 00:31:12.330
And I was like, "I'm just gonna
have some low snake energy

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:14.010
and see what happens."

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:15.577
But it was this way of being like,

00:31:15.577 --> 00:31:18.810
"Okay, how do I witness from down here?

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:20.850
How do I witness from up here?"

00:31:20.850 --> 00:31:23.340
And then I thought about all
the different animals and birds

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:25.140
that are like seeing what they're seeing

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:27.150
from their different viewpoints, right?

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:29.040
The birds that kinda
twist their head and turn

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:30.360
and look at you upside down.

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:33.810
And the trees, how they're
witnessing you from up there

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:34.643
but they're also,

00:31:34.643 --> 00:31:37.020
we're standing on their roots, you know?

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:39.900
They're just underneath us all the time

00:31:39.900 --> 00:31:43.380
and all those different viewpoints
and how that connects us

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:45.990
and how strange it is
that we think it's like,

00:31:45.990 --> 00:31:49.680
just the poet going into the
world being like, "I see this."

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:50.820
- Yeah.
- Right?

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:52.500
But that's not how poetry happens.

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:57.027
It's often like, "Oh, I wonder
what that bird thinks of me?"

00:32:00.692 --> 00:32:03.750
And all of that is in
tandem with, of course,

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:08.580
like how the body is also
nature, and is also animal.

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:13.580
And that feels very important
to my work and my life.

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:16.754
- Thank you.

00:32:16.754 --> 00:32:17.850
(Ada giggles)

00:32:17.850 --> 00:32:19.050
And will you read for us?

00:32:19.050 --> 00:32:20.850
- Yes! I will read for you.

00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:21.683
What if I said no?

00:32:21.683 --> 00:32:22.783
I would be like, "No!"

00:32:22.783 --> 00:32:23.616
(audience laughing)

00:32:23.616 --> 00:32:25.653
Yes, of course I will read.

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:29.160
I shall go to the podium.

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:31.398
It's like a whole
different area over there.

00:32:31.398 --> 00:32:34.440
(audience laughing)

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:35.440
We'll turn this off.

00:32:43.290 --> 00:32:44.280
Can you hear me okay?

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:45.240
- [Audience] Yes.

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:46.073
- Great.

00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:52.657
We were laughing that I was like,

00:32:52.657 --> 00:32:55.740
"Well we can just kind
of, take it as it goes."

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:59.371
And then we realized that
we were both talkers.

00:32:59.371 --> 00:33:00.204
(audience laughing)

00:33:00.204 --> 00:33:02.220
So we're like, "No, we
actually need to keep an eye

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:06.147
on the time to be respectful of everyone."

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:11.430
So I thought I would read,
I think it's gonna be,

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:13.473
I'm gonna guess 10 poems.

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:18.663
I'm gonna give you that number
to make you feel a little,

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:24.360
modicum of control, (laughing)

00:33:24.360 --> 00:33:27.000
in the otherwise uncontrollable situation

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:29.091
that is the poetry reading. (laughing)

00:33:29.091 --> 00:33:30.660
(audience laughing)

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:35.364
You can count down or count
up however you feel like.

00:33:35.364 --> 00:33:38.937
I'm gonna start with this poem
that is in "The Carrying",

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:44.730
which is, I think speaks
to some of the things

00:33:44.730 --> 00:33:46.590
we were just talking about
in that conversation.

00:33:46.590 --> 00:33:50.670
Sort of, looking for some
ways to caretake the self,

00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:54.063
but also sort of denying a kind of wisdom.

00:33:55.767 --> 00:33:58.530
And I like to tell you a little
bit of the background of it,

00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:02.130
which is that it is a poem
that I initially wanted it

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:05.587
to start the collection and my
editor was like, (blows air)

00:34:05.587 --> 00:34:09.183
"No, that's a lot.

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:13.923
And then also I'll say that
it was a prompt that was,

00:34:15.930 --> 00:34:18.390
it was the Academy of American Poets

00:34:18.390 --> 00:34:19.590
asked me to write a poem

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:24.123
that would be the first
poem of the year in 2016.

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:29.760
and of course it was, how do you do that?

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:32.010
They're like, just set
the tone for the year.

00:34:32.864 --> 00:34:33.697
(audience laughing)

00:34:33.697 --> 00:34:36.450
So, and it was a hard year.

00:34:36.450 --> 00:34:39.333
And so this is the poem,

00:34:40.987 --> 00:34:42.027
"The Leash."

00:34:43.537 --> 00:34:46.830
"After the birthing of
bombs of forks and fear,

00:34:46.830 --> 00:34:47.815
the frantic automatic weapons unleashed,

00:34:47.815 --> 00:34:52.815
the spray of bullets into
a crowd holding hands,

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:56.490
that brute sky opening
in a slate metal mall

00:34:56.490 --> 00:35:01.490
that swallows only the unsayable
in each of us, what's left?

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:05.550
Even the hidden nowhere
river is poisoned orange

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:07.950
and acidic by a coal mine.

00:35:07.950 --> 00:35:10.890
How can you not fear humanity?

00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:14.730
Wanna lick the creek bottom
dry to suck the deadly water up

00:35:14.730 --> 00:35:18.270
into your own lungs like venom?

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:23.040
Reader, I wanna say, don't die.

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:25.740
Even when silvery fish after fish

00:35:25.740 --> 00:35:29.430
comes back belly up,
and the country plummets

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:32.310
into a crepitating crater of hatred,

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:34.803
isn't there still something singing?

00:35:35.880 --> 00:35:38.463
The truth is, I don't know.

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:41.640
But sometimes I swear I hear it,

00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:44.940
the wound closing like a
rusted over garage door

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:48.600
and I can still move my
living limbs into the world

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:51.510
without too much pain, can still marvel

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:54.750
at how the dog runs straight
toward the pickup trucks

00:35:54.750 --> 00:35:58.350
break necking down the
road, because she thinks

00:35:58.350 --> 00:36:03.180
she loves them, because
she's sure, without a doubt,

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:07.350
that the loud roaring
things will love her back,

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:10.560
her soft small self alive with desire

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:15.560
to share her goddamn enthusiasm
until I yank the leash back

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:20.840
to save her because I want
her to survive forever.

00:36:21.090 --> 00:36:25.710
Don't die, I say, and we decide
to walk for a bit longer,

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:28.200
starling's high and fevered above us,

00:36:28.200 --> 00:36:31.020
winter coming to lay her cold corpse down

00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:33.630
upon this little plot of earth.

00:36:33.630 --> 00:36:36.360
Perhaps we are always hurdling our bodies

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:39.780
toward the thing that will obliterate us,

00:36:39.780 --> 00:36:43.650
begging for love from the
speeding passage of time,

00:36:43.650 --> 00:36:48.150
and so maybe, like the
dog obedient at my heels,

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:51.120
we can walk together peacefully,

00:36:51.120 --> 00:36:54.657
at least until the next truck comes."

00:36:58.607 --> 00:37:01.792
(audience clapping)

00:37:01.792 --> 00:37:02.625
Thank you.

00:37:04.890 --> 00:37:08.413
Thank you. My dog loves poems about her.

00:37:08.413 --> 00:37:12.240
(audience laughing)

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:14.373
She's a pug, so you know, she's,

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:17.520
everyone's like, "Oh pugs are so funny."

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:18.527
And I was like,

00:37:18.527 --> 00:37:20.945
"No, they're funny because
they're very serious."

00:37:20.945 --> 00:37:23.670
(audience laughing)
(Ada laughing)

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:26.553
And this is a poem that
I wrote for my mother,

00:37:27.840 --> 00:37:31.290
and one of the students
I met with today said

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:33.300
that she read this book with her mother

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:35.673
and so thought I would read this.

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:42.300
The poem that began with the ending for me

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:43.710
and then I built backwards,

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:47.883
which I think has only ever
happened one other time for me.

00:37:50.617 --> 00:37:51.573
"The Raincoat"

00:37:53.227 --> 00:37:55.830
"When the doctor suggested surgery

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:58.470
and a brace for all my youngest years,

00:37:58.470 --> 00:38:01.920
my parents scrambled to
take me to massage therapy,

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:04.470
deep tissue work, osteopathy,

00:38:04.470 --> 00:38:07.920
and soon my crooked spine unspooled a bit,

00:38:07.920 --> 00:38:09.390
I could breathe again

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:13.860
and move more in a body unclouded by pain.

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:16.620
My mom would tell me to sing songs to her

00:38:16.620 --> 00:38:19.890
the whole 45 minute drive
to Middle Two Rock Road

00:38:19.890 --> 00:38:23.460
and 45 minutes back from physical therapy.

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:26.280
She'd say, that even my
voice sounded unfettered

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:28.230
by my spine afterward.

00:38:28.230 --> 00:38:32.880
So I sang and sang, because
I thought she liked it.

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:36.060
I never asked her what
she gave up to drive me

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:39.480
or how her day was before this chore.

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:43.080
Today, at her age, I
was driving myself home

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:45.270
from yet another spine appointment,

00:38:45.270 --> 00:38:49.740
singing along to some maudlin
but solid song on the radio,

00:38:49.740 --> 00:38:53.190
and I saw a mom take her raincoat off

00:38:53.190 --> 00:38:54.780
and give it to her young daughter

00:38:54.780 --> 00:38:57.153
when a storm took over the afternoon.

00:38:58.350 --> 00:39:00.150
My god, I thought,

00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:03.900
my whole life I've been under her raincoat

00:39:03.900 --> 00:39:07.707
thinking it was somehow a
marvel that I never got wet."

00:39:11.823 --> 00:39:14.906
(audience laughing)

00:39:20.490 --> 00:39:25.140
And then I'll read this poem
because it was mentioned

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:30.120
and you can hear the poem
that actually this began with.

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:35.000
A sound, which was the sound of

00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:40.950
recycling bins rolled out altogether

00:39:40.950 --> 00:39:42.513
and how it sounds like thunder?

00:39:43.350 --> 00:39:45.097
You know, there's like that
little moment where you're like,

00:39:45.097 --> 00:39:47.460
"What is that sound?"

00:39:47.460 --> 00:39:49.860
And you are like, "Oh, it's thunder."

00:39:49.860 --> 00:39:51.600
And then you realize
it's just your neighbor

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:53.455
with the recycling bin.

00:39:53.455 --> 00:39:55.380
(audience laughing)

00:39:55.380 --> 00:39:57.080
Of course it's not about that but,

00:39:58.003 --> 00:40:01.503
that's what sort of came in my head.

00:40:03.397 --> 00:40:04.563
"Dead Stars"

00:40:06.637 --> 00:40:10.800
"Out here, there's a bowing
even the trees are doing.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:14.220
Winter's icy hand at
the back of all of us.

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:18.030
Black bark, slick yellow
leaves, a kind of stillness

00:40:18.030 --> 00:40:21.540
that feels so mute it's in another year.

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:26.370
I am a hearth of spiders
these days, a nest of trying.

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:28.710
We point out the stars that make Orion

00:40:28.710 --> 00:40:30.480
as we take out the trash,

00:40:30.480 --> 00:40:34.590
the rolling containers a
song of suburban thunder.

00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:36.540
It's almost romantic

00:40:36.540 --> 00:40:40.920
as we adjust the waxy blue
recycling bin until you say,

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:45.090
'Man, we should really learn
some new constellations.'

00:40:45.090 --> 00:40:46.530
And it's true.

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:49.680
We keep forgetting
about Antlia, Centaurus,

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:54.180
Draco, Lacerta, Hydra, Lyra, Lynx.

00:40:54.180 --> 00:40:57.390
But mostly we're forgetting
we're dead stars too,

00:40:57.390 --> 00:41:01.290
my mouth is full of dust and
I wish to reclaim the rising-

00:41:01.290 --> 00:41:04.500
to lean in the spotlight
of streetlight with you,

00:41:04.500 --> 00:41:07.958
towards what's larger within us.

00:41:07.958 --> 00:41:12.180
Look, we are not unspectacular things.

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:16.530
We've come this far, survived this much.

00:41:16.530 --> 00:41:21.150
What would happen if we
decided to survive more?

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:22.890
To love harder?

00:41:22.890 --> 00:41:27.150
What if we stood up with the
synapses and flesh and said No.

00:41:27.150 --> 00:41:29.040
No, to the rising tides.

00:41:29.040 --> 00:41:32.880
Stood for the many mute
mouths of the sea, the land?

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:35.550
What would happen if we
used our bodies to bargain

00:41:35.550 --> 00:41:38.520
for the safety of others, for earth,

00:41:38.520 --> 00:41:42.870
if we declared a clean night,
if we stopped being terrified,

00:41:42.870 --> 00:41:45.540
if we launched our demands into the sky,

00:41:45.540 --> 00:41:48.633
made ourselves so big
people could point to us

00:41:48.633 --> 00:41:51.600
with the arrows they make in their minds,

00:41:51.600 --> 00:41:55.983
rolling their trash bins out,
after all of this is over?

00:41:59.029 --> 00:42:02.112
(audience clapping)

00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:06.240
And I'll read just one
more from "The Carrying"

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:09.210
and then I'll move on
to "The Hurting Kind."

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:11.070
And you guys can hear me okay?

00:42:11.070 --> 00:42:12.740
Okay, how are you doing out there?

00:42:12.740 --> 00:42:13.693
(audience laughing)

00:42:13.693 --> 00:42:14.943
Are you good? Okay, good.

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:20.650
I had this incredible pleasure of

00:42:23.010 --> 00:42:27.100
going to Mexico City to
be a part of the Fandango

00:42:28.290 --> 00:42:31.140
that was a celebration with Dr. Biden

00:42:31.140 --> 00:42:35.010
and Dr. Gutierrez-Muller,
who's the First Lady of Mexico,

00:42:35.010 --> 00:42:36.780
though she doesn't like

00:42:36.780 --> 00:42:39.090
to be called the First Lady of Mexico,

00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:41.490
she just likes to be
called Dr. Gutierrez-Muller

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:45.720
And she sat next to me on stage

00:42:45.720 --> 00:42:48.780
and read this poem, in English!

00:42:48.780 --> 00:42:49.980
And I got to see her do that

00:42:49.980 --> 00:42:52.353
and it was really emotional for me.

00:42:56.220 --> 00:42:57.900
This is a poem I wrote, yeah,

00:42:57.900 --> 00:43:02.430
as a letter poem for my
dear friend Natalie Diaz.

00:43:02.430 --> 00:43:04.293
It's the first poem in our series.

00:43:05.917 --> 00:43:06.753
"Cargo"

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:11.970
I wish I could write
to you from underwater,

00:43:11.970 --> 00:43:16.410
the warm bath covering my ears-
one of which has three marks

00:43:16.410 --> 00:43:18.870
in the exact shape of a triangle,

00:43:18.870 --> 00:43:21.423
my own atmosphere's asterism.

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:25.200
Last night, the fire-engine
sirens were so loud

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:27.899
they drowned out even the constant bluster

00:43:27.899 --> 00:43:30.720
of the inbound freight trains.

00:43:30.720 --> 00:43:33.240
Did you know, the R. J. Corman Railroad

00:43:33.240 --> 00:43:35.910
runs 500 feet from us?

00:43:35.910 --> 00:43:39.840
Before everything shifted
and I aged into this body,

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:42.990
my grandparents lived
above San Timoteo Canyon,

00:43:42.990 --> 00:43:45.180
where the Southern Pacific Railroad roared

00:43:45.180 --> 00:43:48.120
each scorching California summer day.

00:43:48.120 --> 00:43:52.020
I'd watch for the trains,
howling as they came.

00:43:52.020 --> 00:43:55.230
Manuel is in Chicago today,
and we've both admitted

00:43:55.230 --> 00:43:57.810
that we're travelling
with our passports now.

00:43:57.810 --> 00:43:59.490
Reports of ICE raids

00:43:59.490 --> 00:44:03.120
and both of our bloods are
requiring new medication.

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:06.300
I wish we could go back to the windy dock,

00:44:06.300 --> 00:44:09.600
drinking pink wine and talking smack.

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:12.630
Now it's gray and pitchfork.

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:15.270
The supermarket here
is full of grass seed,

00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:19.320
like spring might actually
come, but I don't know.

00:44:19.320 --> 00:44:20.880
And you?

00:44:20.880 --> 00:44:24.990
I heard from a friend you're
still working on saving words.

00:44:24.990 --> 00:44:27.450
All I've been working on is napping,

00:44:27.450 --> 00:44:31.260
and maybe being kinder
to others, to myself.

00:44:31.260 --> 00:44:33.690
Just this morning, I saw seven cardinals

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:36.780
brash and bold as sin in a leafless tree.

00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:40.050
I let them be for a long
while before I shook the air

00:44:40.050 --> 00:44:43.323
and screwed it all up
just by being alive, too.

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:46.053
Am I braver than those birds?

00:44:47.010 --> 00:44:49.740
Do you ever wonder what the trains carry?

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:54.656
Aluminum ingots, plastic,
brick, corn syrup,

00:44:54.656 --> 00:44:59.550
limestone, fury, alcohol, joy.

00:44:59.550 --> 00:45:03.000
All the world is moving, even sand

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:06.693
from one shore to another
is being shuttled.

00:45:07.680 --> 00:45:12.390
I live my life half afraid,
and half shouting at the trains

00:45:12.390 --> 00:45:14.460
when they thunder by.

00:45:14.460 --> 00:45:16.857
This letter to you is both."

00:45:19.208 --> 00:45:22.291
(audience clapping)

00:45:23.245 --> 00:45:24.078
Thank you.

00:45:25.490 --> 00:45:30.148
If you were counting, just six more to go.

00:45:30.148 --> 00:45:33.231
(audience laughing)

00:45:37.470 --> 00:45:38.710
Begin with this poem

00:45:40.800 --> 00:45:44.733
for my stepmother who passed away in 2010,

00:45:47.250 --> 00:45:48.543
a lovely human being.

00:45:49.470 --> 00:45:53.760
And also one of my favorite plants,

00:45:53.760 --> 00:45:56.220
which is one of the first signs of spring.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:57.540
It's probably here too.

00:45:57.540 --> 00:45:59.460
You have Forsythia?

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:04.460
Yeah, it's a prose poem so it
reads a little differently.

00:46:05.491 --> 00:46:07.013
I'll read it so you can hear the sound.

00:46:08.257 --> 00:46:09.153
"Forsythia"

00:46:10.507 --> 00:46:14.490
"At the cabin in Snug Hollow
near McSwain Branch creek,

00:46:14.490 --> 00:46:16.920
just spring, all the animals are out,

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:20.700
and my beloved and I are lying
in bed in a soft silence.

00:46:20.700 --> 00:46:24.240
We are talking about how we
carry so many people with us

00:46:24.240 --> 00:46:27.570
wherever we go, how
even when simply living,

00:46:27.570 --> 00:46:31.410
these unearned moments,
are a tribute to the dead.

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:34.860
We are both expecting to hear
an owl as the night deepens.

00:46:34.860 --> 00:46:38.850
All afternoon, from the porch,
we watched an eastern towhee

00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:42.720
furiously build her nest
in the untamed forsythia

00:46:42.720 --> 00:46:46.410
with its yellow spilling
out onto the horizon.

00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:50.520
I told him that the way I
remember the name forsythia

00:46:50.520 --> 00:46:53.640
is that when my stepmother,
Cynthia, was dying,

00:46:53.640 --> 00:46:57.600
that last week, she said
lucidly but mysteriously,

00:46:57.600 --> 00:47:00.030
'More yellow.'

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:03.120
And I thought yes, more yellow,

00:47:03.120 --> 00:47:05.190
and nodded because I agreed.

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:07.050
Of course, more yellow.

00:47:07.050 --> 00:47:11.013
And so now in my head, when I
see that yellow tangle, I say,

00:47:11.013 --> 00:47:13.650
'For Cynthia, for Cynthia,

00:47:13.650 --> 00:47:17.880
forsythia, forsythia, more yellow.'

00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:19.290
It is night now.

00:47:19.290 --> 00:47:23.100
And the owl never comes,
only more of night

00:47:23.100 --> 00:47:25.523
and what repeats in the night.

00:47:27.445 --> 00:47:30.528
(audience clapping)

00:47:35.190 --> 00:47:37.233
That one always does something to me.

00:47:39.180 --> 00:47:41.594
I say that like someone else wrote it.

00:47:41.594 --> 00:47:42.427
(audience laughing)

00:47:42.427 --> 00:47:46.263
I was like, whew, that
one really gets to me.

00:47:47.439 --> 00:47:50.106
(Ada chuckling)

00:47:52.530 --> 00:47:53.363
Let's see,

00:47:56.970 --> 00:47:57.920
I wanna read a poem

00:48:01.140 --> 00:48:01.973
about

00:48:04.710 --> 00:48:05.970
rethinking old myths.

00:48:05.970 --> 00:48:07.945
We were talking about that before

00:48:07.945 --> 00:48:09.030
and I think that there's,

00:48:09.030 --> 00:48:11.310
it's important sometimes
to just let go of things

00:48:11.310 --> 00:48:14.160
that people have told
you or you've held onto

00:48:14.160 --> 00:48:19.160
that feels like just not true

00:48:19.170 --> 00:48:21.093
or maybe just not useful anymore.

00:48:21.990 --> 00:48:23.047
I used to have people who would say,

00:48:23.047 --> 00:48:26.130
"Oh, you always do this"
and I think I am like that.

00:48:26.130 --> 00:48:30.237
And then five years later I
was like, "I'm not like that."

00:48:32.310 --> 00:48:37.310
And this is a time when right
when my parents got divorced,

00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:38.970
I had a woman,

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:41.437
a mother of a friend of
mine come up and say,

00:48:41.437 --> 00:48:44.097
"I'm so sorry you're
from a broken home now."

00:48:45.264 --> 00:48:47.847
And I remember thinking,
"Oh, my home is broken?"

00:48:48.750 --> 00:48:53.400
Like I didn't, I thought
it seemed okay actually.

00:48:53.400 --> 00:48:56.583
I was like, "They seem a
lot happier, to be honest."

00:48:56.583 --> 00:48:58.260
(audience laughing)

00:48:58.260 --> 00:49:00.300
Anyway, so right in the middle

00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:02.100
of the early days of the pandemic,

00:49:02.100 --> 00:49:05.373
when we still weren't seeing
anyone masked or unmasked,

00:49:06.750 --> 00:49:09.783
this poem came to me with the first line,

00:49:11.947 --> 00:49:14.257
"Joint Custody."

00:49:14.257 --> 00:49:19.257
"Why did I never see it
for what it was, abundance.

00:49:19.980 --> 00:49:23.010
Two families, two
different kitchen tables,

00:49:23.010 --> 00:49:27.630
two sets of rules, two creeks,
two highways, two stepparents

00:49:27.630 --> 00:49:31.080
with their fish tanks or eight
tracks or cigarette smoke

00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:35.130
or expertise in recipes or reading skills.

00:49:35.130 --> 00:49:38.220
I cannot reverse it, the record scratched

00:49:38.220 --> 00:49:41.610
and stopping to that
original chaotic track.

00:49:41.610 --> 00:49:45.930
But let me say, I was taken
back and forth on Sundays

00:49:45.930 --> 00:49:50.930
and it was not easy but
I was loved each place.

00:49:50.940 --> 00:49:53.700
And so I have two brains now.

00:49:53.700 --> 00:49:56.130
Two entirely different brains.

00:49:56.130 --> 00:49:58.800
The one that always misses where I'm not,

00:49:58.800 --> 00:50:02.757
and the one that is so
relieved to finally be home."

00:50:05.829 --> 00:50:08.912
(audience clapping)

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:17.520
And then I'll read this poem.

00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:20.613
You know, maybe like many of you,

00:50:21.660 --> 00:50:26.553
if you're having a bad day or
you're stressed or anxious,

00:50:28.590 --> 00:50:30.697
you just watch animal videos

00:50:30.697 --> 00:50:31.680
(audience laughing)

00:50:31.680 --> 00:50:33.840
and it's just like you
just like push replay.

00:50:33.840 --> 00:50:36.690
Like I need to see that
bird doing that thing again

00:50:36.690 --> 00:50:40.800
or that cat eating the most
cat food it possibly can

00:50:40.800 --> 00:50:41.973
out of the bowl.

00:50:43.980 --> 00:50:47.103
Or whales, I love just videos of whales.

00:50:49.590 --> 00:50:51.483
This is one about a mountain lion.

00:50:52.950 --> 00:50:54.930
And of course oftentimes
I say this is about this.

00:50:54.930 --> 00:50:56.490
And then there's a lot of other things

00:50:56.490 --> 00:50:58.347
that are at play I hope, (chuckles)

00:50:58.347 --> 00:50:59.250
(audience laughing)

00:50:59.250 --> 00:51:04.250
I'm feel like I'm being very
reductive to my own poem,

00:51:07.207 --> 00:51:08.547
"The Mountain Lion."

00:51:10.027 --> 00:51:12.900
"I watched the video clip over and over,

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:17.742
night vision cameras flickering
her eyes an unholy green,

00:51:17.742 --> 00:51:21.840
the way she looked the
six-foot fence up and down

00:51:21.840 --> 00:51:24.990
like it was nothing but a speed bump

00:51:24.990 --> 00:51:29.990
then cleared the man-made
border in one impressive leap.

00:51:30.810 --> 00:51:33.900
A glance over the shoulder, an annoyance,

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:38.343
an, as if you could keep
me out, or keep me in.

00:51:39.900 --> 00:51:41.400
I don't know what it was

00:51:41.400 --> 00:51:45.840
that made me press replay and replay.

00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:49.530
Not fear, though I'd be
terrified if I was face to face

00:51:49.530 --> 00:51:52.530
with her, or heard her
prowling in the night.

00:51:52.530 --> 00:51:54.540
It was just that I don't think

00:51:54.540 --> 00:51:58.890
I've ever made anything look so easy.

00:51:58.890 --> 00:52:01.590
Never looked behind me
and grinned or grimaced

00:52:01.590 --> 00:52:04.470
because nothing could stop me.

00:52:04.470 --> 00:52:06.810
I like the idea of it though,

00:52:06.810 --> 00:52:09.963
felt like a dream you
could will into being.

00:52:10.920 --> 00:52:13.677
See a fence? Jump it."

00:52:14.523 --> 00:52:17.563
(scattered audience gasps)

00:52:17.563 --> 00:52:20.646
(audience clapping)

00:52:27.734 --> 00:52:29.317
I'll read this poem

00:52:33.475 --> 00:52:34.600
for my grandfather

00:52:36.840 --> 00:52:41.840
and my grandfather on my father's side,

00:52:43.830 --> 00:52:48.423
my paternal grandfather, abuelo paternal.

00:52:49.650 --> 00:52:54.650
And he was (speaks Spanish)

00:52:55.620 --> 00:53:00.620
And he came from Mexico
when he was quite young

00:53:00.750 --> 00:53:02.343
and was a foster child.

00:53:05.910 --> 00:53:08.910
I read this poem because I
wanna bring him into this space

00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:12.330
and also think about all
the people that were artists

00:53:12.330 --> 00:53:15.213
but maybe didn't get a
chance to be artists.

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:20.220
And I think sometimes we can
feel like we're making art

00:53:20.220 --> 00:53:24.180
and it maybe it's like I feel
like I need to keep this poem

00:53:24.180 --> 00:53:27.030
in my heart when I'm like
running through the airport.

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:29.040
You know? Very glamorous life.

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:31.507
Trying to like glide into
the plane being like,

00:53:31.507 --> 00:53:33.147
"Don't leave without me!"

00:53:34.440 --> 00:53:39.327
And be like, "I'm doing this
because I get to do this."

00:53:40.290 --> 00:53:41.910
And I think sometimes we need to say that

00:53:41.910 --> 00:53:43.623
about lots of things in our lives.

00:53:45.547 --> 00:53:47.187
"Heart on Fire."

00:53:48.817 --> 00:53:51.570
"As a foster child, my grandfather learned

00:53:51.570 --> 00:53:53.430
not to get in trouble.

00:53:53.430 --> 00:53:57.840
Mexican and motherless, dead
as she was from tuberculosis,

00:53:57.840 --> 00:53:59.940
he practiced words in a new language

00:53:59.940 --> 00:54:02.550
and kept his slender head down.

00:54:02.550 --> 00:54:03.960
When the other boys begged him

00:54:03.960 --> 00:54:06.720
to slip into the music shop's upper window

00:54:06.720 --> 00:54:11.310
to steal harmonicas for each
of them, music being important,

00:54:11.310 --> 00:54:15.120
thievery being secondary, he refused.

00:54:15.120 --> 00:54:16.950
When the cops came to spot the boys

00:54:16.950 --> 00:54:20.340
who robbed the music store,
they could easily find the ones

00:54:20.340 --> 00:54:22.890
spitting broken notes into the air,

00:54:22.890 --> 00:54:25.440
joyously mouthing the stainless steel,

00:54:25.440 --> 00:54:28.623
mimicking men on street
corners busking for coins.

00:54:29.490 --> 00:54:33.000
But not my grandfather,
he knew not to risk it all

00:54:33.000 --> 00:54:36.150
for a stolen moment of exaltation.

00:54:36.150 --> 00:54:39.900
It's easy to imagine
this is who I come from,

00:54:39.900 --> 00:54:44.900
a line of serious men who follow
the rules, but might I add

00:54:45.360 --> 00:54:50.070
that later he was a
dancer, a singer, an actor

00:54:50.070 --> 00:54:53.520
whose best roles ended up
on the cutting room floor.

00:54:53.520 --> 00:54:57.300
A cutup, a ham who liked a good story.

00:54:57.300 --> 00:55:00.660
Who would have told you life
was a series of warnings,

00:55:00.660 --> 00:55:03.120
but also magic.

00:55:03.120 --> 00:55:06.090
Once, he was sent for a box of matches

00:55:06.090 --> 00:55:08.610
and he put that box of strike-anywheres

00:55:08.610 --> 00:55:12.690
in the pocket of his
madras shirt and ran home,

00:55:12.690 --> 00:55:17.010
he ran so fast to be on time, to be good,

00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:21.810
and when he did so, the whole box ignited,

00:55:21.810 --> 00:55:25.200
so he was a boy running
down the canyon road

00:55:25.200 --> 00:55:28.920
with what looked like a heart on fire.

00:55:28.920 --> 00:55:31.440
He'd laugh when he told you this,

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:36.393
'A heart on fire,' he'd
say, so you'd remember.

00:55:40.015 --> 00:55:43.098
(audience clapping)

00:55:47.610 --> 00:55:49.953
And then I'll read just three more poems.

00:56:03.013 --> 00:56:03.963
This is a poem that

00:56:06.720 --> 00:56:10.890
I wrote actually for Greenpeace.

00:56:10.890 --> 00:56:15.603
And it was right after the
fires in Sonoma of 2017.

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:25.460
It was on a thread on
Twitter the other day

00:56:25.500 --> 00:56:29.460
and it was all these apology
poems and it was great.

00:56:29.460 --> 00:56:30.630
I went on it on the plane

00:56:30.630 --> 00:56:32.970
and it was like all of
these people were sharing

00:56:32.970 --> 00:56:35.910
their favorite poems that
had apologies in them.

00:56:35.910 --> 00:56:36.840
And I was like, what a great,

00:56:36.840 --> 00:56:40.203
you could teach a whole course
on just an apology poem.

00:56:41.280 --> 00:56:42.113
So I thought I would read this

00:56:42.113 --> 00:56:43.863
'cause it reminded me of my own poem.

00:56:46.507 --> 00:56:47.583
"Salvage"

00:56:49.117 --> 00:56:51.540
"On the top of Mount Pisgah,

00:56:51.540 --> 00:56:54.210
on the western slope of the Mayacamas,

00:56:54.210 --> 00:56:57.900
there's a madrone tree that's
half-burned from the fires,

00:56:57.900 --> 00:57:02.070
half-alive from nature's
need to propagate.

00:57:02.070 --> 00:57:05.190
One side of her is black ash

00:57:05.190 --> 00:57:08.220
and at her root is what
looks like a cavity

00:57:08.220 --> 00:57:10.710
hollowed out by flame.

00:57:10.710 --> 00:57:14.850
On the other side, silvery
green broadleaf shoots

00:57:14.850 --> 00:57:17.250
ascend toward the winter light

00:57:17.250 --> 00:57:20.040
and her bark is a cross
between a bay horse

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:22.020
and a chestnut horse,

00:57:22.020 --> 00:57:25.713
red and velvety like the
animal's neck she resembles.

00:57:26.760 --> 00:57:30.060
Staring at the tree for a long time now.

00:57:30.060 --> 00:57:33.510
I am reminded of the righteousness I had

00:57:33.510 --> 00:57:35.940
before the scorch of time.

00:57:35.940 --> 00:57:38.250
I miss who I was.

00:57:38.250 --> 00:57:42.480
I miss who we all were,
before we were this,

00:57:42.480 --> 00:57:47.340
half alive to the brightening
sky, half dead already.

00:57:47.340 --> 00:57:52.020
I place my hand on the
unscarred bark that is cool

00:57:52.020 --> 00:57:57.020
and unsullied, and because I
cannot apologize to the tree,

00:57:57.840 --> 00:58:02.136
to my own self I say, 'I am sorry.

00:58:02.136 --> 00:58:06.620
I am sorry I have been so
reckless with your life.'"

00:58:09.143 --> 00:58:12.143
(audience clapping)

00:58:18.930 --> 00:58:22.683
Let's see here, I'll read this poem.

00:58:26.220 --> 00:58:27.573
That's another prose poem.

00:58:28.884 --> 00:58:31.134
Do you guys know the
instrument the jaw bone?

00:58:32.310 --> 00:58:36.570
Let's say it's an actual
jawbone of a horse or a donkey

00:58:36.570 --> 00:58:40.143
and it makes most amazing music.

00:58:42.307 --> 00:58:43.707
"Instrumentation."

00:58:45.067 --> 00:58:48.150
"If I could ever play
an instrument for real,

00:58:48.150 --> 00:58:51.090
I like the idea of playing the jawbone,

00:58:51.090 --> 00:58:53.640
the rattle of something
dead in your hands.

00:58:53.640 --> 00:58:56.640
The thing that beats
back at the sky and says,

00:58:56.640 --> 00:58:58.290
'I'm still here.'

00:58:58.290 --> 00:59:01.140
Even though clearly the donkey isn't here

00:59:01.140 --> 00:59:03.360
or the horse isn't here.

00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:07.320
Just the teeth and the jaw
making music like resurrection

00:59:07.320 --> 00:59:09.483
or haunting or just plain need.

00:59:10.350 --> 00:59:14.820
What I like most is that
the jawbone is an idiophone,

00:59:14.820 --> 00:59:18.090
which I misread once as idea phone.

00:59:18.090 --> 00:59:21.390
But an idiophone is
just that it makes music

00:59:21.390 --> 00:59:25.260
by the whole thing
vibrating without strings.

00:59:25.260 --> 00:59:28.770
I want that, that kind
of reeling in the wind.

00:59:28.770 --> 00:59:32.700
All the loose dry teeth, all
the old bones of the skull,

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:35.850
all the world and the figure
swaying with its stick

00:59:35.850 --> 00:59:40.113
to make untuned music
even death cannot deny.

00:59:42.918 --> 00:59:46.001
(audience clapping)

00:59:49.452 --> 00:59:50.820
And I'll close with this poem

00:59:50.820 --> 00:59:53.253
and then if you have any
questions you can ask.

00:59:54.166 --> 00:59:56.100
This is a poem that I wrote

00:59:56.100 --> 01:00:00.210
during the very early days of the pandemic

01:00:00.210 --> 01:00:04.560
and people would call me
and ask me about, you know,

01:00:04.560 --> 01:00:07.860
isn't it true that we need
poetry now more than ever?

01:00:07.860 --> 01:00:10.203
And I was like, is it true?

01:00:11.700 --> 01:00:16.080
I feel like we need lots
of things like healthcare

01:00:16.080 --> 01:00:19.710
(audience laughing)
and a vaccine

01:00:19.710 --> 01:00:21.850
and (laughing)

01:00:23.700 --> 01:00:25.413
different modes of support.

01:00:26.488 --> 01:00:30.360
But while this was happening,
I was also making a list

01:00:30.360 --> 01:00:31.770
of subjects that I felt like,

01:00:31.770 --> 01:00:34.880
I'm certain many of you who
are writers know this feeling

01:00:34.880 --> 01:00:37.470
where I've already written about
that I can't write it again

01:00:37.470 --> 01:00:41.400
or I've done that before and
so it just became this list

01:00:41.400 --> 01:00:42.993
of things in my head.

01:00:44.010 --> 01:00:47.523
And then of course what happened
was it turned into a poem,

01:00:50.767 --> 01:00:52.617
"The End of Poetry."

01:00:54.240 --> 01:00:57.840
Enough of osseous and
chickadee and sunflower

01:00:57.840 --> 01:01:02.700
and snowshoes, maple and
seeds, samara and shoot,

01:01:02.700 --> 01:01:05.880
enough chiaroscuro, enough
of thus and prophecy

01:01:05.880 --> 01:01:08.100
and the stoic farmer
and faith and our father

01:01:08.100 --> 01:01:11.100
and tis of thee, enough of bosom and bud,

01:01:11.100 --> 01:01:12.750
skin and god not forgetting

01:01:12.750 --> 01:01:15.360
and star bodies and frozen birds,

01:01:15.360 --> 01:01:17.580
enough of the will to go on and not go on

01:01:17.580 --> 01:01:21.390
or how a certain light
does a certain thing,

01:01:21.390 --> 01:01:23.040
enough of the kneeling and the rising

01:01:23.040 --> 01:01:25.560
and the looking inward and the looking up,

01:01:25.560 --> 01:01:27.600
enough of the gun, the drama,

01:01:27.600 --> 01:01:29.370
and the acquaintance's suicide,

01:01:29.370 --> 01:01:31.470
the long-lost letter on the dresser,

01:01:31.470 --> 01:01:33.210
enough of the longing and the ego

01:01:33.210 --> 01:01:35.520
and the obliteration of ego,

01:01:35.520 --> 01:01:37.350
enough of the mother and the child

01:01:37.350 --> 01:01:39.120
and the father and the child

01:01:39.120 --> 01:01:41.220
and enough of the pointing to the world,

01:01:41.220 --> 01:01:44.850
weary and desperate, enough
of the brutal and the border,

01:01:44.850 --> 01:01:47.310
enough of can you see me, can you hear me,

01:01:47.310 --> 01:01:50.970
enough I am human, enough I
am alone and I am desperate,

01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:54.660
enough of the animal saving
me, enough of the high water,

01:01:54.660 --> 01:01:58.230
enough sorrow, enough
of the air and its ease,

01:01:58.230 --> 01:02:00.753
I am asking you to touch me.

01:02:02.610 --> 01:02:03.516
Thank you.

01:02:03.516 --> 01:02:06.599
(audience clapping)

01:02:31.470 --> 01:02:35.470
- [Alicia] Come forward
and direct the question.

01:02:36.845 --> 01:02:38.213
- [Host] Please cue in the center aisle,

01:02:39.600 --> 01:02:40.710
a few minutes before six,

01:02:40.710 --> 01:02:42.830
we'll cue you that last question or two,

01:02:42.830 --> 01:02:44.747
if you have a question.

01:02:49.920 --> 01:02:53.272
- I know it's pressure
'cause it's like a microphone

01:02:53.272 --> 01:02:54.609
and it's right there.

01:02:54.609 --> 01:02:57.692
(audience laughing)

01:03:05.010 --> 01:03:07.743
Oh yes, A brave one.

01:03:08.970 --> 01:03:11.310
- [Audience Member] What
is your favorite poem?

01:03:11.310 --> 01:03:14.340
- My favorite poem of all time?

01:03:14.340 --> 01:03:16.680
That's a great question.

01:03:16.680 --> 01:03:21.315
It changes because sometimes
I need a certain poem

01:03:21.315 --> 01:03:23.250
and that poem becomes my favorite.

01:03:23.250 --> 01:03:25.440
And then sometimes I need another poem.

01:03:25.440 --> 01:03:29.250
But I think Elizabeth Bishop's "One Art"

01:03:29.250 --> 01:03:30.843
might be my favorite poem.

01:03:33.330 --> 01:03:34.280
Thank you for that.

01:03:38.260 --> 01:03:40.620
(audience clapping)

01:03:40.620 --> 01:03:42.660
- [Audience Member] Is
this working? Oh, okay.

01:03:42.660 --> 01:03:43.863
This is so satisfying.

01:03:44.854 --> 01:03:46.590
Hi.
(audience laughing)

01:03:46.590 --> 01:03:47.423
First of all,

01:03:48.502 --> 01:03:50.837
like this reading is like the
highlight of my week, so I'm.

01:03:50.837 --> 01:03:53.224
- I'm so glad. Thank you.

01:03:53.224 --> 01:03:54.131
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:03:54.131 --> 01:03:55.860
I'm, it was amazing.

01:03:55.860 --> 01:03:58.980
Especially like the
last poem, I think like,

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:00.090
there's this Instagram account

01:04:00.090 --> 01:04:01.790
called Poetry is Not
Luxury and I'm pretty sure,

01:04:01.790 --> 01:04:06.790
I feel like I saw that on there
and now I wanna read more.

01:04:06.810 --> 01:04:07.650
But like, anyways,

01:04:07.650 --> 01:04:09.243
so like to get to the point,

01:04:10.710 --> 01:04:14.144
I'm wondering what you think about cliches

01:04:14.144 --> 01:04:17.290
and like what the role of
cliches has been your work

01:04:18.975 --> 01:04:21.180
and if there's ever a point
where you're just like,

01:04:21.180 --> 01:04:23.530
have I written everything
I will ever write

01:04:26.562 --> 01:04:28.530
for the end of time?

01:04:28.530 --> 01:04:30.720
Like how do you pick
yourself up from this space

01:04:30.720 --> 01:04:34.620
that like,

01:04:34.620 --> 01:04:38.190
artistic paralysis and
like working with cliches

01:04:38.190 --> 01:04:41.460
or maybe working with like
being more direct in your poetry

01:04:41.460 --> 01:04:44.880
and like maybe swapping
figurative language for just like,

01:04:44.880 --> 01:04:47.983
this is how I'm feeling
viscerally right now

01:04:47.983 --> 01:04:51.270
and I just need to get it out on a page.

01:04:51.270 --> 01:04:52.890
- Yeah, that's a great question.

01:04:52.890 --> 01:04:54.480
I really appreciate that.

01:04:54.480 --> 01:04:57.030
I'll say that there's two things
that I'll say is that one,

01:04:57.030 --> 01:05:02.030
I feel like it's all about interrogation.

01:05:03.540 --> 01:05:06.360
So it feels to me like
when there's a moment

01:05:06.360 --> 01:05:09.330
where I'm just saying the
direct thing or it feels direct,

01:05:09.330 --> 01:05:11.790
I ask myself like, is that direct?

01:05:11.790 --> 01:05:14.580
Is that the thing? Or
is there more going on?

01:05:14.580 --> 01:05:18.060
And sometimes the figurative
language comes into that

01:05:18.060 --> 01:05:21.150
or the imagistic language or
maybe it just needs sounds

01:05:21.150 --> 01:05:24.060
or something that will
actually explain it further

01:05:24.060 --> 01:05:25.350
or elucidate it more

01:05:25.350 --> 01:05:27.750
so that it's not just the feeling anymore

01:05:27.750 --> 01:05:28.890
or the straightforward thing,

01:05:28.890 --> 01:05:32.670
but that it builds into
something that's just my own.

01:05:32.670 --> 01:05:33.990
And that is something.

01:05:33.990 --> 01:05:35.730
So it's like always asking the thing,

01:05:35.730 --> 01:05:36.600
you know, is this true?

01:05:36.600 --> 01:05:38.400
Is this really, is this true?

01:05:38.400 --> 01:05:40.290
And then the other part
I will say is that,

01:05:40.290 --> 01:05:43.740
especially with cliches,
is that I love cliches.

01:05:43.740 --> 01:05:46.590
I think they're wonderful
and they exist for a reason.

01:05:46.590 --> 01:05:49.080
And then what's really fun
about them is to tug them

01:05:49.080 --> 01:05:51.907
and pull them apart to start going like,

01:05:51.907 --> 01:05:55.991
"Okay, so why did we say
I have a poem that's about

01:05:55.991 --> 01:06:00.329
that idea of it's always
in the last place you look

01:06:00.329 --> 01:06:02.610
and how silly that is."

01:06:02.610 --> 01:06:04.960
Like, well of course it
is 'cause you found it.

01:06:08.340 --> 01:06:10.980
But I like the poem,
sometimes just taking a cliche

01:06:10.980 --> 01:06:13.470
that someone says and
then pulling it apart

01:06:13.470 --> 01:06:14.610
and looking at it.

01:06:14.610 --> 01:06:15.870
In my book, "Bright Dead Things,"

01:06:15.870 --> 01:06:18.000
I have a poem called "Downhearted"

01:06:18.000 --> 01:06:18.835
and it's about this idea

01:06:18.835 --> 01:06:22.560
of picturing the heart actually down

01:06:22.560 --> 01:06:25.560
and that it's sunk to
the bottom of your torso,

01:06:25.560 --> 01:06:26.880
that's downhearted

01:06:26.880 --> 01:06:29.460
and that it's on a couch

01:06:29.460 --> 01:06:31.800
pulling up the blankets over its head.

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:33.510
So I think sometimes it's just about

01:06:33.510 --> 01:06:36.993
interrogating the cliche
and then breaking it apart.

01:06:39.648 --> 01:06:40.481
- [Audience Member] Oh, thank you so much.

01:06:40.481 --> 01:06:41.530
- Yeah, of course.

01:06:41.530 --> 01:06:45.120
(audience clapping)

01:06:45.120 --> 01:06:46.890
- [Audience Member] I had a
class and I showed up very late.

01:06:46.890 --> 01:06:48.150
So I apologize if I'm out of breath

01:06:48.150 --> 01:06:51.180
and if this was covered at the start,

01:06:51.180 --> 01:06:56.013
but as someone who's not a
poet and sort of admires poets,

01:06:58.650 --> 01:07:01.260
could you describe a little
bit what the editing process,

01:07:01.260 --> 01:07:04.140
specifically for a poet of
your caliber, looks like?

01:07:04.140 --> 01:07:08.400
Because I think in the
few interactions I've had

01:07:08.400 --> 01:07:10.710
with poets my age, who are very talented,

01:07:10.710 --> 01:07:13.830
it feels very different
than pro's editing.

01:07:13.830 --> 01:07:17.737
And also my imagination tells me that

01:07:19.620 --> 01:07:22.410
at least when I'm editing pros,

01:07:22.410 --> 01:07:26.160
I kind of know what I'm writing
about from the beginning.

01:07:26.160 --> 01:07:27.810
But poetry, I feel like there are times

01:07:27.810 --> 01:07:29.006
when a new day brings

01:07:29.006 --> 01:07:32.943
sort of a new perspective, a new image.

01:07:34.380 --> 01:07:35.213
What does that look like?

01:07:35.213 --> 01:07:36.243
- Yeah, that's great.

01:07:37.500 --> 01:07:38.333
- [Audience Member] How do you know

01:07:38.333 --> 01:07:39.280
when a poem is done?

01:07:39.280 --> 01:07:41.340
Which also feels more
tricky than perhaps pros.

01:07:41.340 --> 01:07:43.650
- Yeah, these are both great questions.

01:07:43.650 --> 01:07:48.650
Revision to me is actually,
I think, really fun.

01:07:48.870 --> 01:07:53.220
But it is primarily where
the writing happens in poetry

01:07:53.220 --> 01:07:55.590
because we're working with such tiny units

01:07:55.590 --> 01:07:58.620
it's so small that
everything is compressed

01:07:58.620 --> 01:08:02.280
and everything is sort of
vibrating with the intensity

01:08:02.280 --> 01:08:05.970
of that white space around
it, that silence around it

01:08:05.970 --> 01:08:08.430
so that it has to become,

01:08:08.430 --> 01:08:11.460
sometimes it feels like you have a tweezer

01:08:11.460 --> 01:08:15.180
and you're just moving pieces
of rice around the page,

01:08:15.180 --> 01:08:18.390
but they're very important
pieces of rice, you know?

01:08:18.390 --> 01:08:23.390
But I think for me, a lot of
it's about the sound quality.

01:08:23.550 --> 01:08:26.970
And so I compose out
loud and I edit out loud

01:08:26.970 --> 01:08:28.950
so that I'm reading the
poem over and over again.

01:08:28.950 --> 01:08:30.210
I'm listening for places

01:08:30.210 --> 01:08:33.225
where I've either cut the line too short

01:08:33.225 --> 01:08:35.880
and the breath doesn't catch up with it,

01:08:35.880 --> 01:08:37.890
or I stumble on it each time.

01:08:37.890 --> 01:08:39.330
Or I'm looking for a place where I'm like,

01:08:39.330 --> 01:08:40.533
oh, this is actually,

01:08:41.702 --> 01:08:44.070
I love sound work, so it's easy for me

01:08:44.070 --> 01:08:46.470
to kind of get into the
consonance and assonance

01:08:47.453 --> 01:08:49.897
and all the fun alliteration
things, and then be like,

01:08:49.897 --> 01:08:53.100
"Okay, calm down Ada,
you know you don't need

01:08:53.100 --> 01:08:58.100
to do acrobatics right now,
just move the machine forward."

01:09:00.120 --> 01:09:02.130
So a lot of that is sound work.

01:09:02.130 --> 01:09:05.013
And then the other part is
looking to figure out if,

01:09:05.879 --> 01:09:07.380
like when you asked about,

01:09:07.380 --> 01:09:09.420
coming to it like a
different day and being like,

01:09:09.420 --> 01:09:11.430
well, what was the intention?

01:09:11.430 --> 01:09:12.843
Oftentimes I'm asking, okay,

01:09:13.759 --> 01:09:15.270
what is the intention
of this original poem?

01:09:15.270 --> 01:09:18.990
Like what is original draft
and how can I stay true

01:09:18.990 --> 01:09:20.980
to what the poem wants to do

01:09:20.980 --> 01:09:24.300
without it becoming just sort
of where I want it to go?

01:09:24.300 --> 01:09:26.100
Or maybe I started with this idea

01:09:26.100 --> 01:09:29.010
that I wanted to write a poem about this,

01:09:29.010 --> 01:09:31.770
but then the poem wanted to do
something entirely different.

01:09:31.770 --> 01:09:35.220
And usually what happens is
when I revise and I fail,

01:09:35.220 --> 01:09:36.480
it's because I'm like, "No, no, no,

01:09:36.480 --> 01:09:38.370
I wanted to write a poem about this,"

01:09:38.370 --> 01:09:41.610
but I'm not looking at what
the poem is actually doing.

01:09:41.610 --> 01:09:44.340
And the poem is often wiser than we are.

01:09:44.340 --> 01:09:47.400
And it feels like the poem
really isn't happening

01:09:47.400 --> 01:09:48.930
until it has taught us something,

01:09:48.930 --> 01:09:50.160
which the opposite of prose,

01:09:50.160 --> 01:09:52.620
where you start with this
idea, and you're like,

01:09:52.620 --> 01:09:54.870
okay, I kind of kind of
know where I'm going,

01:09:54.870 --> 01:09:56.460
even though you, of course,
you learn as you go,

01:09:56.460 --> 01:09:57.420
and that's why we write.

01:09:57.420 --> 01:09:59.820
But there is this level
in which with the poem,

01:09:59.820 --> 01:10:01.987
it really does sometimes
just come at you and go,

01:10:01.987 --> 01:10:04.500
"Oh, I didn't know that this was

01:10:04.500 --> 01:10:06.597
what it was supposed to teach me."

01:10:07.800 --> 01:10:10.740
And so I think that moment has to happen.

01:10:10.740 --> 01:10:14.130
And that's also similar to
like how I know it's done,

01:10:14.130 --> 01:10:16.290
is that there's something that feels,

01:10:16.290 --> 01:10:19.800
maybe not like a
conclusion, but a wholeness,

01:10:19.800 --> 01:10:23.280
a completeness, something that feels like

01:10:23.280 --> 01:10:26.613
whatever it began as, it has fulfilled.

01:10:28.890 --> 01:10:30.107
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much.

01:10:30.107 --> 01:10:31.190
- Thank you.

01:10:31.190 --> 01:10:33.627
(audience clapping)

01:10:33.627 --> 01:10:35.280
- [Audience Member] Hi,
thank you for being here.

01:10:35.280 --> 01:10:37.020
I have a related question.

01:10:37.020 --> 01:10:39.030
I read somewhere, and I hope this is true

01:10:39.030 --> 01:10:40.920
and I'm not making it up,

01:10:40.920 --> 01:10:44.700
that you started to write
fiction and realized somehow

01:10:44.700 --> 01:10:47.400
that you were a poet and
not a fiction writer.

01:10:47.400 --> 01:10:49.770
And I'd love to hear
more about that moment

01:10:49.770 --> 01:10:51.210
and what that felt like

01:10:51.210 --> 01:10:54.330
and what exactly tipped
you into that conclusion.

01:10:54.330 --> 01:10:58.003
- Okay, so not entirely off base,

01:10:58.003 --> 01:10:59.100
(audience laughing)

01:10:59.100 --> 01:11:03.720
but I'll say this, I was always a poet.

01:11:03.720 --> 01:11:06.630
I had three books out and
then I decided I wanted

01:11:06.630 --> 01:11:09.480
to try my hand at fiction.

01:11:09.480 --> 01:11:11.767
And so I, you know, quit
my job and I was like,

01:11:11.767 --> 01:11:13.557
"I'm gonna be a full-time writer,"

01:11:14.550 --> 01:11:16.830
which I don't know why I
thought I could do that,

01:11:16.830 --> 01:11:19.623
but I guess it turned out okay actually.

01:11:20.794 --> 01:11:24.690
But I moved out of New York
and moved back to California

01:11:24.690 --> 01:11:26.493
and I did write a novel.

01:11:27.530 --> 01:11:31.210
Well, I mean many drafts
of a failed novel or a

01:11:34.080 --> 01:11:37.906
project that I learned a lot from.

01:11:37.906 --> 01:11:39.557
(audience laughing)

01:11:39.557 --> 01:11:44.100
And as I was writing that novel,

01:11:44.100 --> 01:11:46.410
what happened was I was
spending so much time

01:11:46.410 --> 01:11:49.380
in the life of other characters,
which I wanted to do,

01:11:49.380 --> 01:11:52.043
because after three books
of poetry, I was like,

01:11:52.043 --> 01:11:53.730
"Okay, that's like
enough of myself," right?

01:11:53.730 --> 01:11:57.120
And then what happened was
then during the evenings,

01:11:57.120 --> 01:11:59.160
I would still kind of feel
like writing something,

01:11:59.160 --> 01:12:01.590
but I would wanna write
these observations.

01:12:01.590 --> 01:12:03.780
I would wanna write a
little bit about myself.

01:12:03.780 --> 01:12:08.010
And that's really how the fourth
book, "Bright Dead Things",

01:12:08.010 --> 01:12:11.550
came into being, which was,
it was almost like a byproduct

01:12:11.550 --> 01:12:13.500
of this novel I was working on

01:12:13.500 --> 01:12:16.260
because I was storing up all
these poems inside of me,

01:12:16.260 --> 01:12:19.260
and they would just come out
like fully formed all the time.

01:12:20.220 --> 01:12:21.528
So, yeah.

01:12:21.528 --> 01:12:24.870
So you're not, I had always
started as a poet, but I did,

01:12:24.870 --> 01:12:29.250
that book does really kind
of take some of its life form

01:12:29.250 --> 01:12:30.870
from the novel.

01:12:30.870 --> 01:12:31.703
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:12:31.703 --> 01:12:33.443
- Yeah, of course.

01:12:33.443 --> 01:12:36.540
(audience clapping)

01:12:36.540 --> 01:12:37.373
- [Audience Member] Hi there.

01:12:37.373 --> 01:12:38.910
Thank you so much for
being here and doing this.

01:12:38.910 --> 01:12:40.391
This was really lovely.

01:12:40.391 --> 01:12:41.670
- Yeah. Thank you.

01:12:41.670 --> 01:12:43.470
- [Audience Member] I was wondering,

01:12:44.329 --> 01:12:45.960
in your appointment as Poet Laureate,

01:12:45.960 --> 01:12:47.793
and all the books you've had,

01:12:47.793 --> 01:12:50.862
I'd imagine those are
very codifying experiences

01:12:50.862 --> 01:12:52.740
in sort of your identity
as a poet and as a writer.

01:12:52.740 --> 01:12:55.740
And so I was wondering
what sort of inspired you

01:12:55.740 --> 01:12:58.080
to keep creating and to keep writing

01:12:58.080 --> 01:12:59.610
in moments when you've
maybe had more doubt

01:12:59.610 --> 01:13:02.733
about your place as a writer
or as a creative person?

01:13:03.630 --> 01:13:05.220
- Yeah, you know,

01:13:05.220 --> 01:13:06.660
I think that there's a lot of times

01:13:06.660 --> 01:13:09.600
where it's very easy
to get down on oneself,

01:13:09.600 --> 01:13:13.950
no matter what you do, and
it happens to me still.

01:13:13.950 --> 01:13:15.817
And there was moments where I think,

01:13:15.817 --> 01:13:20.817
"Oh, well, now that this
appointment has happened,

01:13:21.480 --> 01:13:22.470
I'll never write again."

01:13:22.470 --> 01:13:24.450
It's very easy for me
to turn anything into,

01:13:24.450 --> 01:13:25.904
I'll just never write again.

01:13:25.904 --> 01:13:27.690
(audience laughing)
And sometimes,

01:13:27.690 --> 01:13:29.880
like if I write a poem
I'm really excited about

01:13:29.880 --> 01:13:31.440
and people are like, "This is great,"

01:13:31.440 --> 01:13:34.800
as soon as I get very elated, you know,

01:13:34.800 --> 01:13:37.267
I pour myself a cocktail and
two seconds later I'm like,

01:13:37.267 --> 01:13:39.720
"Well, I guess that's the
last poem I've ever written."

01:13:39.720 --> 01:13:41.700
Like, I just, I don't know what it is.

01:13:41.700 --> 01:13:42.870
So I think we all do it.

01:13:42.870 --> 01:13:46.320
We have this kind of self-talk
that just is like, you know,

01:13:46.320 --> 01:13:49.920
and I think as I have aged,
I do think I've trusted it.

01:13:49.920 --> 01:13:52.950
Like now it's like,
okay, I'm six books in,

01:13:52.950 --> 01:13:55.560
they will come and they work in seasons,

01:13:55.560 --> 01:13:59.310
and I work in seasons so
that now I trust the silence

01:13:59.310 --> 01:14:01.410
so that if it isn't coming,

01:14:01.410 --> 01:14:05.160
I just think it's gonna come
and it's gonna come, you know,

01:14:05.160 --> 01:14:07.010
for me, I love writing in the spring,

01:14:08.010 --> 01:14:11.277
so right now, all the
trees are kind of sleeping,

01:14:11.277 --> 01:14:15.000
they're starting to figure
out what they're gonna do,

01:14:15.000 --> 01:14:16.680
that's kind of where I'm at.

01:14:16.680 --> 01:14:18.330
And then things will come.

01:14:18.330 --> 01:14:20.580
So it's a lot about trusting that.

01:14:20.580 --> 01:14:22.830
I think the other thing
I think about is that

01:14:24.090 --> 01:14:24.933
when people say,

01:14:26.407 --> 01:14:28.223
"How could you write
through a time like this?"

01:14:28.223 --> 01:14:29.410
Or how it's like really hard right now

01:14:29.410 --> 01:14:32.100
where I think about like, you know,

01:14:32.100 --> 01:14:36.180
Phyllis Wheatley writing as
a enslaved woman, you know?

01:14:36.180 --> 01:14:41.180
I think about Sor Juana Inés
de la Cruz writing, you know,

01:14:41.610 --> 01:14:43.170
in Mexico all those years ago,

01:14:43.170 --> 01:14:45.510
and like holding her manuscript
to my hands thinking,

01:14:45.510 --> 01:14:48.480
this is what she created at this time.

01:14:48.480 --> 01:14:51.810
So I'm always like trying to
look at the legacy of others

01:14:51.810 --> 01:14:53.040
and how that can inspire us.

01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:55.860
Especially when I feel myself
kind of getting down on,

01:14:55.860 --> 01:14:58.743
you know, either myself or art in general.

01:14:59.580 --> 01:15:02.250
And I always pull myself
up by thinking about

01:15:02.250 --> 01:15:07.113
how many people have done so
much in such harrowing times.

01:15:08.670 --> 01:15:09.503
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:15:09.503 --> 01:15:11.122
- Thank you.

01:15:11.122 --> 01:15:12.960
(audience clapping)

01:15:12.960 --> 01:15:14.244
- [Audience Member] Hi,

01:15:14.244 --> 01:15:15.090
thank you so much for being here today.

01:15:15.090 --> 01:15:16.174
- Thank you.

01:15:16.174 --> 01:15:17.335
- [Audience Member] I had a question

01:15:17.335 --> 01:15:18.210
about your background in theater,

01:15:18.210 --> 01:15:20.220
because theater is so much about, I guess,

01:15:20.220 --> 01:15:21.150
becoming a different character

01:15:21.150 --> 01:15:22.170
and becoming a different person,

01:15:22.170 --> 01:15:23.910
whereas poetry is about honesty.

01:15:23.910 --> 01:15:27.360
And in theater you wanna
sound natural and up on stage,

01:15:27.360 --> 01:15:29.670
but in poetry sometimes you
like to play with language

01:15:29.670 --> 01:15:30.750
and do different things.

01:15:30.750 --> 01:15:33.492
So what was that transition like from you

01:15:33.492 --> 01:15:34.590
and do you think theater influences

01:15:34.590 --> 01:15:36.459
how you write poetry or vice versa?

01:15:36.459 --> 01:15:37.740
- That's a great question.

01:15:37.740 --> 01:15:40.170
I have never once regretted

01:15:40.170 --> 01:15:42.270
my undergraduate degree in theater.

01:15:42.270 --> 01:15:44.576
It was a great experience for me.

01:15:44.576 --> 01:15:46.320
I searched on campus,

01:15:46.320 --> 01:15:48.480
I went to the University
of Washington in Seattle,

01:15:48.480 --> 01:15:49.950
and I remember, you know,

01:15:49.950 --> 01:15:52.602
I had come from a town
that was 30,000 people

01:15:52.602 --> 01:15:55.615
and I went to a campus
that was 40,000 people.

01:15:55.615 --> 01:15:58.590
And I was like, where are my people?

01:15:58.590 --> 01:16:00.000
I didn't know, I didn't know what to do.

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:02.886
And I remember sort of walking
into the theater department

01:16:02.886 --> 01:16:05.490
and everyone was like just
sort of running around

01:16:05.490 --> 01:16:07.290
and like barefoot and laughing

01:16:07.290 --> 01:16:09.720
and someone was like
singing opera on the corner.

01:16:09.720 --> 01:16:12.267
And I was like, "Oh, I
think I belong here."

01:16:13.590 --> 01:16:14.730
And it just felt like this.

01:16:14.730 --> 01:16:16.830
We were, you know,
everyone was a little bit,

01:16:16.830 --> 01:16:19.110
everyone was interested in making things.

01:16:19.110 --> 01:16:22.320
And I think that was actually
probably more healthy for me

01:16:22.320 --> 01:16:27.180
at the time than it was in
the creative writing program

01:16:27.180 --> 01:16:29.220
because I didn't have that background.

01:16:29.220 --> 01:16:30.770
It was a little more, you know,

01:16:32.220 --> 01:16:34.020
I think it would've
been I too intimidating

01:16:34.020 --> 01:16:35.720
and it might have even put me off.

01:16:36.660 --> 01:16:38.700
But, so for me, I think
one of the big things

01:16:38.700 --> 01:16:41.370
was that theater teaches you about breath.

01:16:41.370 --> 01:16:45.120
It teaches you about communication
and it teaches you about

01:16:45.120 --> 01:16:49.260
what it is to sort of stand
in your being, you know,

01:16:49.260 --> 01:16:50.640
and be present.

01:16:50.640 --> 01:16:53.430
And if the best stage actors can do that

01:16:53.430 --> 01:16:55.950
and really embody someone else.

01:16:55.950 --> 01:16:57.600
And I think the other part of it was

01:16:57.600 --> 01:17:01.440
doing monologues really taught
me a lot about what it was

01:17:01.440 --> 01:17:04.350
to write something that
could turn and twist and move

01:17:04.350 --> 01:17:07.890
in a short space.

01:17:07.890 --> 01:17:08.810
And then the last thing I'll say is,

01:17:08.810 --> 01:17:13.110
is that when I did take poetry

01:17:13.110 --> 01:17:15.750
in my junior and senior years,

01:17:15.750 --> 01:17:18.570
because I had exhausted
the electives I could take

01:17:18.570 --> 01:17:20.670
inside the theater
program, they were like,

01:17:20.670 --> 01:17:21.813
you need to move on.

01:17:22.920 --> 01:17:26.767
I went and I took these
classes and I was like,

01:17:26.767 --> 01:17:29.040
"Oh, I love this."

01:17:29.040 --> 01:17:31.470
And I had also realized
that for the most part,

01:17:31.470 --> 01:17:34.290
I was speaking the words of others, right?

01:17:34.290 --> 01:17:35.220
That's what theater is.

01:17:35.220 --> 01:17:39.000
And I was also at that time
really primarily working

01:17:39.000 --> 01:17:42.052
with the words of older white men.

01:17:42.052 --> 01:17:45.090
And so it felt like I had this opportunity

01:17:45.090 --> 01:17:47.940
to then write myself into the world.

01:17:47.940 --> 01:17:51.360
And it really felt like freedom.

01:17:51.360 --> 01:17:54.960
And I think once I found
that kind of freeing space,

01:17:54.960 --> 01:17:57.273
I thought, okay, poetry is it.

01:17:58.800 --> 01:18:00.453
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much.

01:18:00.453 --> 01:18:01.286
Yeah, thank you.

01:18:01.286 --> 01:18:04.530
(audience clapping)

01:18:04.530 --> 01:18:05.363
- [Audience Member] Hi.

01:18:05.363 --> 01:18:09.180
I'm wondering what your
relationship is like

01:18:09.180 --> 01:18:13.290
to the speaker of your
poems, and if six books in

01:18:13.290 --> 01:18:15.273
that's changed for you?

01:18:16.470 --> 01:18:17.423
- Yeah, that's a great question.

01:18:17.423 --> 01:18:19.770
I think in order to sort
of protect each other

01:18:19.770 --> 01:18:23.190
as we talk about work, we
always use the speaker,

01:18:23.190 --> 01:18:24.540
the speaker in the poem is doing this,

01:18:24.540 --> 01:18:26.910
the speaker in the poem
is this. And you know,

01:18:26.910 --> 01:18:30.723
when I sit down to the
page, I'm the speaker,

01:18:31.680 --> 01:18:35.100
And so I feel like it's
okay to conflate the two.

01:18:35.100 --> 01:18:38.130
I think sometimes it's
nice to separate that

01:18:38.130 --> 01:18:39.810
when we talk about someone else's work

01:18:39.810 --> 01:18:41.490
or even if we need to for ourselves,

01:18:41.490 --> 01:18:43.040
like the speaker is doing this.

01:18:44.442 --> 01:18:46.710
For the most part, I'm very comfortable

01:18:46.710 --> 01:18:48.640
with it just being me

01:18:50.400 --> 01:18:52.470
and feeling in some ways

01:18:52.470 --> 01:18:56.040
that actually the more
human I can get on the page,

01:18:56.040 --> 01:19:00.333
the more interesting the
poem might be for myself

01:19:00.333 --> 01:19:01.803
as I'm making it.

01:19:02.760 --> 01:19:04.020
So I'm trying to actually look

01:19:04.020 --> 01:19:08.403
for a little even more intimacy
beyond the word speaker.

01:19:09.960 --> 01:19:12.360
But that doesn't mean
sometimes I still wanna keep it

01:19:12.360 --> 01:19:13.503
as self protection.

01:19:14.917 --> 01:19:15.873
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:19:15.873 --> 01:19:17.030
- Yeah.

01:19:17.030 --> 01:19:20.022
(audience clapping)

01:19:20.022 --> 01:19:20.855
- [Audience Member] Hi.

01:19:20.855 --> 01:19:22.860
Thank you so much for
the beautiful reading.

01:19:22.860 --> 01:19:23.693
- Thank you.

01:19:23.693 --> 01:19:24.680
- [Audience Member] I just wanted to ask,

01:19:24.680 --> 01:19:29.680
as someone who I find needs
poetry to properly enjoy life

01:19:31.050 --> 01:19:33.780
and love life, have you ever had

01:19:33.780 --> 01:19:37.050
and someone who finds it
difficult when I can't write

01:19:37.050 --> 01:19:38.370
what I need to write? Mm.

01:19:38.370 --> 01:19:40.140
Or I am afraid to,

01:19:40.140 --> 01:19:42.720
have you ever had moments
in which you feel like you

01:19:42.720 --> 01:19:44.310
need to write something but you can't

01:19:44.310 --> 01:19:46.470
or you are afraid to write something?

01:19:46.470 --> 01:19:49.680
And how did you get through
that and how did that feel?

01:19:49.680 --> 01:19:51.930
- Yeah, that's a great question.

01:19:51.930 --> 01:19:53.640
Yeah, there have definitely been times

01:19:53.640 --> 01:19:56.367
where I felt like I haven't
wanted to write something

01:19:56.367 --> 01:19:59.730
or felt like I shouldn't write something.

01:19:59.730 --> 01:20:00.990
Who am I to write that?

01:20:00.990 --> 01:20:05.990
Or how might this do harm to those I love.

01:20:07.140 --> 01:20:09.030
That's probably one of the
biggest things is like,

01:20:09.030 --> 01:20:11.583
could this harm someone close to me?

01:20:12.630 --> 01:20:16.440
Is it my story to tell or
is it a joint story? Right?

01:20:16.440 --> 01:20:19.710
Is it a family story that's not just mine?

01:20:19.710 --> 01:20:22.350
There's a lot of those
questions of ethics.

01:20:22.350 --> 01:20:23.790
And I'll be honest

01:20:23.790 --> 01:20:25.560
that I don't think we
talk about it enough,

01:20:25.560 --> 01:20:26.880
because I think that what happens is,

01:20:26.880 --> 01:20:29.460
sometimes we as writers
are like, I'm the writer.

01:20:29.460 --> 01:20:31.410
I should be able to have
all the freedom I want

01:20:31.410 --> 01:20:32.820
to write about whatever I want.

01:20:32.820 --> 01:20:33.810
Which is a beautiful thing.

01:20:33.810 --> 01:20:34.830
If you're not a human being

01:20:34.830 --> 01:20:37.290
that lives with other human beings,

01:20:37.290 --> 01:20:40.080
or if you've never had
a human relationship,

01:20:40.080 --> 01:20:41.820
then that's fine.

01:20:41.820 --> 01:20:44.730
But I think that, if we want
our writing to have impact,

01:20:44.730 --> 01:20:45.720
then we also have to be aware

01:20:45.720 --> 01:20:47.310
that writing can have consequences.

01:20:47.310 --> 01:20:49.877
And I think that we
actually should learn that

01:20:49.877 --> 01:20:53.850
because you can write the hard thing,

01:20:53.850 --> 01:20:56.463
but unless you're ready
to have that conversation,

01:20:57.390 --> 01:20:59.040
then don't share it.

01:20:59.040 --> 01:21:00.660
You might need to write it for yourself

01:21:00.660 --> 01:21:02.820
and you might need to put it in a drawer.

01:21:02.820 --> 01:21:06.750
And I know lots of incredible
poets who have written books,

01:21:06.750 --> 01:21:10.140
upon books that are on shelves

01:21:10.140 --> 01:21:14.283
and will probably be published
when someone passes away.

01:21:15.210 --> 01:21:17.850
And I think that that's okay.

01:21:17.850 --> 01:21:19.560
And I think that unless you're ready

01:21:19.560 --> 01:21:22.500
to do some deep deeper excavation,

01:21:22.500 --> 01:21:24.750
or you're ready to approach
whatever subject it is

01:21:24.750 --> 01:21:26.200
that might be terrifying you,

01:21:27.510 --> 01:21:30.120
then you may not be ready
to share it with the world.

01:21:30.120 --> 01:21:35.100
And that is okay, forcing it never works.

01:21:35.100 --> 01:21:37.680
It never works. So I
think it's always good.

01:21:37.680 --> 01:21:40.020
Again, the goal is longevity, right?

01:21:40.020 --> 01:21:43.050
We want you to write and we
want you to keep writing.

01:21:43.050 --> 01:21:44.137
We don't want you to write and be like,

01:21:44.137 --> 01:21:45.630
"Oh, well that was traumatic.

01:21:45.630 --> 01:21:47.700
Let's not do that again."

01:21:47.700 --> 01:21:51.540
Yeah, so I think it's always
a way of protecting yourself,

01:21:51.540 --> 01:21:55.020
encouraging yourself, but
making sure that you continue.

01:21:55.020 --> 01:21:55.853
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much.

01:21:55.853 --> 01:21:56.834
- Yeah.

01:21:56.834 --> 01:22:00.187
(audience clapping)

01:22:00.187 --> 01:22:01.020
- [Audience Member] Hi.

01:22:01.020 --> 01:22:02.280
I'm probably like the
hundredth participants person

01:22:02.280 --> 01:22:04.314
to say thank you, but
thank you for coming.

01:22:04.314 --> 01:22:06.060
- You're welcome. Thank you.

01:22:06.060 --> 01:22:07.650
- [Audience Member] You
were talking about earlier,

01:22:07.650 --> 01:22:09.960
at the beginning how you pushed back

01:22:09.960 --> 01:22:11.850
against a myth of making art alone

01:22:11.850 --> 01:22:16.850
and I wanted to ask if there was ever,

01:22:17.340 --> 01:22:19.690
if there was a moment or
if there was a process

01:22:20.595 --> 01:22:22.110
that made you realize that it was a myth.

01:22:22.110 --> 01:22:25.290
Because I feel like it's
very easy to succumb to it

01:22:25.290 --> 01:22:28.980
and it's much harder to
internalize that, that feeling.

01:22:28.980 --> 01:22:30.210
Also, what's your pugs name?

01:22:30.210 --> 01:22:32.310
- Yeah. Okay.
(audience laughing)

01:22:32.310 --> 01:22:33.143
Well first of all,

01:22:33.143 --> 01:22:36.951
my pug is named Lily
Bean Kudzu Mark Limon.

01:22:36.951 --> 01:22:38.662
(audience laughing)

01:22:38.662 --> 01:22:40.104
- [Audience Member] Good name.

01:22:40.104 --> 01:22:41.463
- And she is amazing.

01:22:42.360 --> 01:22:45.930
And she used to travel
with me, but she's 12 now.

01:22:45.930 --> 01:22:46.998
And I feel like she just,

01:22:46.998 --> 01:22:48.990
all she does is snore and sleep

01:22:48.990 --> 01:22:51.090
and it would just be actually distracting

01:22:51.090 --> 01:22:52.653
'cause her snore is so loud.

01:22:53.497 --> 01:22:57.000
But yeah, she, she's fantastic.

01:22:57.000 --> 01:22:59.037
Okay. Well, the other question,

01:22:59.037 --> 01:23:01.080
I just got distracted by my pug.

01:23:01.080 --> 01:23:03.900
Remind me what you just said before that.

01:23:03.900 --> 01:23:04.980
See, I got distracted.

01:23:04.980 --> 01:23:06.870
I was like, my pug.

01:23:06.870 --> 01:23:08.610
- [Audience Member] It's
okay. Happens to me too.

01:23:08.610 --> 01:23:11.580
Just if there was a moment
or a specific process.

01:23:11.580 --> 01:23:12.690
- Oh, yes, yes.

01:23:12.690 --> 01:23:14.493
You know what it was, it was that,

01:23:15.768 --> 01:23:18.780
you sort of, you read literary biographies

01:23:18.780 --> 01:23:22.560
or you look at these poets
that have hung out together

01:23:22.560 --> 01:23:25.320
or novelists or artist
and you realize like, oh,

01:23:25.320 --> 01:23:28.735
all these people were hanging
out together, you know?

01:23:28.735 --> 01:23:30.930
Where someone's like,
you meet with someone

01:23:30.930 --> 01:23:32.790
and they're like, "Oh yeah,
no, that was my teacher."

01:23:32.790 --> 01:23:33.849
You know?

01:23:33.849 --> 01:23:35.730
I was like, I always loved Mur Kaiser.

01:23:35.730 --> 01:23:37.170
And I remember I studied with Sharon Olds

01:23:37.170 --> 01:23:39.660
and Sharon Olds was like, "Yeah,
Mur Kaiser was my teacher."

01:23:39.660 --> 01:23:42.030
And I was like, oh, right,

01:23:42.030 --> 01:23:44.580
Like this is, this is what this is.

01:23:44.580 --> 01:23:48.120
Like, there is so many cycles
and so many communities

01:23:48.120 --> 01:23:51.900
and we think of like,
so-and-so being on a pedestal

01:23:51.900 --> 01:23:54.510
or this and that and I
just don't think it's true.

01:23:54.510 --> 01:23:57.000
And then other thing
is that it's like, yes,

01:23:57.000 --> 01:23:59.283
do we have to actually do the work alone?

01:24:00.150 --> 01:24:02.790
Yeah, for the most part you do.

01:24:02.790 --> 01:24:04.337
That's actually one of the
things I like about it,

01:24:04.337 --> 01:24:06.450
'cause it takes a lot to do theater,

01:24:06.450 --> 01:24:09.090
it takes a lot of people to make theater.

01:24:09.090 --> 01:24:13.443
I get to do my art alone in
my house, in my slippers.

01:24:14.610 --> 01:24:16.470
So I think there is that part,

01:24:16.470 --> 01:24:18.450
but I think it's really,

01:24:18.450 --> 01:24:20.040
I think it was that moment
was in graduate school,

01:24:20.040 --> 01:24:22.590
just watching everyone
kind of come together

01:24:22.590 --> 01:24:23.910
and be in community.

01:24:23.910 --> 01:24:27.930
I didn't have that in the
creative writing as an undergrad.

01:24:27.930 --> 01:24:28.860
I had that in theater,

01:24:28.860 --> 01:24:30.960
but I didn't know it in creative writing.

01:24:30.960 --> 01:24:32.850
But yeah, it came as a graduate school.

01:24:32.850 --> 01:24:35.147
- [Audience Member]
Great. Thank you so much.

01:24:35.147 --> 01:24:35.980
- Yeah.

01:24:35.980 --> 01:24:38.839
(audience clapping)

01:24:38.839 --> 01:24:39.750
- Hi.
- Hi.

01:24:39.750 --> 01:24:41.370
- Thank you so much again.

01:24:41.370 --> 01:24:42.203
This was great.

01:24:42.203 --> 01:24:43.199
- Thank you.

01:24:43.199 --> 01:24:45.963
- [Audience Member] So I wanted to ask,

01:24:47.025 --> 01:24:50.160
I'm going to generalize
a lot, so I apologize.

01:24:50.160 --> 01:24:51.990
But I noticed in your poetry

01:24:51.990 --> 01:24:55.800
that on one hand you read
a lot about the body,

01:24:55.800 --> 01:24:58.470
and on the other hand a lot about nature.

01:24:58.470 --> 01:25:01.410
And I was wondering how you
conceptualize the relationship

01:25:01.410 --> 01:25:04.020
between the two, is it a boundary,

01:25:04.020 --> 01:25:05.790
is it a continuity, is it a mirror?

01:25:05.790 --> 01:25:08.730
Like, or is it all of those
things or none of those things?

01:25:08.730 --> 01:25:10.110
- Yeah.

01:25:10.110 --> 01:25:10.943
I love that question.

01:25:10.943 --> 01:25:14.313
I think for me, it kind
of depends on the day.

01:25:15.420 --> 01:25:17.613
You know, sometimes I'm like, oh,

01:25:19.663 --> 01:25:22.470
this feels like I am in tandem
and connected to everything.

01:25:22.470 --> 01:25:24.180
And sometimes I feel like, oh,

01:25:24.180 --> 01:25:28.090
I am a machine that is
walking around doing things

01:25:29.182 --> 01:25:31.770
with a head full of thoughts
that is very separate, right?

01:25:31.770 --> 01:25:34.140
And I'm always trying
to bring them together.

01:25:34.140 --> 01:25:35.098
And then there are
moments where I feel like,

01:25:35.098 --> 01:25:39.330
oh, I am just as much
animal as that squirrel,

01:25:39.330 --> 01:25:41.818
and why am I wearing clothes?

01:25:41.818 --> 01:25:43.890
(audience laughing)

01:25:43.890 --> 01:25:46.690
Because it's weird that we're
animals that wear clothes.

01:25:47.820 --> 01:25:49.080
(audience laughing)

01:25:49.080 --> 01:25:50.550
So all of those things.

01:25:50.550 --> 01:25:51.743
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:25:54.169 --> 01:25:56.610
(audience clapping)

01:25:56.610 --> 01:25:58.710
- [Audience Member] Hi,
thank you so much for coming.

01:25:58.710 --> 01:25:59.820
Your reading was very moving.

01:25:59.820 --> 01:26:02.970
I was reading "The Hurting
Kind" like a few days ago

01:26:02.970 --> 01:26:04.740
at the basement of one of our libraries.

01:26:04.740 --> 01:26:06.840
And I remember sitting
there for 10 minutes,

01:26:06.840 --> 01:26:10.620
but I kind of wanted to
ask about your writing.

01:26:10.620 --> 01:26:14.700
And partly because I think a
lot of what you write about

01:26:14.700 --> 01:26:18.990
resonates with me in
themes of grief and loss.

01:26:18.990 --> 01:26:20.940
And sometimes as a poet,

01:26:20.940 --> 01:26:25.940
I personally have had issues
like writing about grief

01:26:26.310 --> 01:26:28.890
or loss that isn't my own.

01:26:28.890 --> 01:26:31.530
So the only time I have been
able to write or express myself

01:26:31.530 --> 01:26:34.380
or release my pain has
been through poetry,

01:26:34.380 --> 01:26:35.940
but it has been hard for me

01:26:35.940 --> 01:26:39.690
to reconstruct someone else's
pain and then write about it.

01:26:39.690 --> 01:26:42.600
And so I was wondering like,
is that something you've done?

01:26:42.600 --> 01:26:46.340
Is that something you find
an avenue of poetry from?

01:26:46.340 --> 01:26:47.850
And yeah, just want your thoughts.

01:26:47.850 --> 01:26:50.910
- Yeah, I think for me, for the most part,

01:26:50.910 --> 01:26:53.463
when I'm dealing with
a grief or a lost poem,

01:26:54.780 --> 01:26:57.510
it feels like it's either very personal

01:26:57.510 --> 01:27:00.870
or it's very collective,

01:27:00.870 --> 01:27:04.620
and I have to have some
part of that belongs to me,

01:27:04.620 --> 01:27:07.743
some part of it that feels
like, oh, this is also mine.

01:27:09.000 --> 01:27:11.730
And whether that's
national grief, you know,

01:27:11.730 --> 01:27:13.413
whether that's communal grief.

01:27:14.610 --> 01:27:18.360
And so I have to find my
thread and connection to it.

01:27:18.360 --> 01:27:20.760
If it's someone else's,
I may not approach it.

01:27:20.760 --> 01:27:22.413
That's me as an artist.

01:27:23.430 --> 01:27:25.530
And that's also because I'm very wary

01:27:25.530 --> 01:27:28.560
of appropriating someone else's pain

01:27:28.560 --> 01:27:30.843
in order to perpetuate art.

01:27:32.580 --> 01:27:36.090
Not everyone feels that way, but I do.

01:27:36.090 --> 01:27:38.280
So I feel like there's
a way in which that I,

01:27:38.280 --> 01:27:41.610
it has to feel like it's some
thread of it belongs to me

01:27:41.610 --> 01:27:43.980
enough so that I can have
an intimate experience

01:27:43.980 --> 01:27:45.180
in the making of it,

01:27:45.180 --> 01:27:47.613
and that I feel urgently connected to it.

01:27:48.810 --> 01:27:50.860
Otherwise it might feel a little too much

01:27:51.744 --> 01:27:54.720
like a project or a manipulation.

01:27:54.720 --> 01:27:55.553
Yeah.

01:27:55.553 --> 01:27:57.167
- Thank you so much.
- Yeah. Thank you.

01:27:57.167 --> 01:28:00.250
(audience clapping)

01:28:01.620 --> 01:28:03.233
- [Audience Member] Thank you for coming.

01:28:04.353 --> 01:28:05.550
And kind of similar to the last question

01:28:06.875 --> 01:28:10.800
of having experiences of
grief and pain and loss,

01:28:10.800 --> 01:28:14.220
but then wanting to let
those experiences go,

01:28:14.220 --> 01:28:19.050
but at the same time, wanting to create

01:28:19.050 --> 01:28:23.340
and how there's this situation
where you want to let go,

01:28:23.340 --> 01:28:26.637
but then you also want to
create something fasting.

01:28:27.663 --> 01:28:28.763
And how do you basically, how
do you deal with that problem?

01:28:31.980 --> 01:28:36.603
- Yeah, I think that for
me you have to get curious.

01:28:40.140 --> 01:28:44.313
So I find myself in those
situations asking myself,

01:28:45.180 --> 01:28:49.263
what is it that wants to hold onto this?

01:28:50.400 --> 01:28:51.573
Why this moment?

01:28:52.560 --> 01:28:55.590
Why did this person come
back to me right now?

01:28:55.590 --> 01:29:00.120
Why does this feel almost like, you know,

01:29:00.120 --> 01:29:02.313
something worth living for this pain?

01:29:03.660 --> 01:29:07.110
I have to get very curious
about those things.

01:29:07.110 --> 01:29:12.110
And it's asking yourself,
okay, is there a lesson here?

01:29:12.180 --> 01:29:14.640
Is there something that this
is supposed to teach me?

01:29:14.640 --> 01:29:17.040
What's the gift I got from this?

01:29:17.040 --> 01:29:19.470
You know, it's starting
to ask those questions

01:29:19.470 --> 01:29:21.363
about those losses,

01:29:22.350 --> 01:29:25.020
and in doing so unraveling why it is

01:29:25.020 --> 01:29:27.663
that they're so
instrumental to who we are.

01:29:28.530 --> 01:29:29.580
- Thank you.
- Yeah.

01:29:30.720 --> 01:29:33.780
(audience clapping)

01:29:33.780 --> 01:29:34.613
- Hi.
- Hi.

01:29:34.613 --> 01:29:35.697
- [Audience Member] Oh my God.

01:29:36.690 --> 01:29:39.990
So you mentioned your friend
of yours named Natalie Diaz,

01:29:39.990 --> 01:29:41.400
and am I a correct in assuming

01:29:41.400 --> 01:29:43.080
that's the author of
Post-Colonial Love poem?

01:29:43.080 --> 01:29:44.010
- Yes.

01:29:44.010 --> 01:29:46.080
- Love that book. Love yours as well.

01:29:46.080 --> 01:29:49.380
- Yeah, she has,

01:29:49.380 --> 01:29:52.710
so Post-Colonial has the
other set of the poems

01:29:52.710 --> 01:29:54.325
that are written to each other.

01:29:54.325 --> 01:29:56.100
- Oh that's fantastic.
- Yeah.

01:29:56.100 --> 01:29:57.720
- [Audience Member] I'll
go back and look for it.

01:29:57.720 --> 01:29:58.896
But there's this line from
Postcolonial Love Poem

01:29:58.896 --> 01:30:01.293
that I think about always,

01:30:02.243 --> 01:30:03.990
and I'm definitely not gonna
be quoting it word for word,

01:30:03.990 --> 01:30:05.730
but it goes something like,

01:30:05.730 --> 01:30:08.400
it is real work not to perform a fable,

01:30:08.400 --> 01:30:11.010
just ask the turtle, ask the hare.

01:30:11.010 --> 01:30:13.680
And I kind of wanted to ask
you whose poems I've read

01:30:13.680 --> 01:30:15.300
and seem so free and liberating

01:30:15.300 --> 01:30:18.480
and have this sense of
reverence for what is small,

01:30:18.480 --> 01:30:19.887
both small and beautiful.

01:30:19.887 --> 01:30:23.610
And I kind of wanted to ask how
you negotiate those terms as

01:30:23.610 --> 01:30:27.063
someone who maybe does exist
in within the marginalized,

01:30:29.103 --> 01:30:29.936
and yeah, just as a writer myself,

01:30:29.936 --> 01:30:32.310
I would love to emulate
some of those freedoms

01:30:32.310 --> 01:30:34.020
and hear your process.

01:30:34.020 --> 01:30:36.195
- Yeah. I think that's really beautiful.

01:30:36.195 --> 01:30:37.920
And it's a great question to end on

01:30:37.920 --> 01:30:41.970
because I think it's really
essential to how we all work

01:30:41.970 --> 01:30:44.100
I think that, as I mentioned earlier

01:30:44.100 --> 01:30:47.790
about like how I like to push
against the unhelpful myths.

01:30:47.790 --> 01:30:50.483
The things that are like,
this is not helpful to me,

01:30:50.483 --> 01:30:52.470
this is not useful to me.

01:30:52.470 --> 01:30:54.840
I think in the same way that
as soon as someone wants

01:30:54.840 --> 01:30:59.760
to tell me that I need to write
about some specific thing,

01:30:59.760 --> 01:31:00.997
I will immediately be like,

01:31:00.997 --> 01:31:03.300
"No, I do not need to do that."

01:31:03.300 --> 01:31:05.430
And there's this like, wickedness in me

01:31:05.430 --> 01:31:08.880
that immediately wants
to deny any part of,

01:31:08.880 --> 01:31:12.330
like, when someone is like,
"Oh, you're a Mexican,

01:31:12.330 --> 01:31:17.040
so where's your abuela poem,"
and all of these things.

01:31:17.040 --> 01:31:19.503
And immediately I'm
like, no, no, no, no, no,

01:31:20.800 --> 01:31:21.633
I'm gonna write about this tree

01:31:21.633 --> 01:31:23.310
and that's what I'm gonna
write about, you know?

01:31:23.310 --> 01:31:24.600
And then sometimes like, you know,

01:31:24.600 --> 01:31:27.360
six years later I'll be like,
okay, here's my grandpa poem,

01:31:27.360 --> 01:31:28.740
or here's my grandma poem.

01:31:28.740 --> 01:31:31.593
But there's these ways
in which that I think,

01:31:34.492 --> 01:31:38.550
I'm very suspicious of
what is asked of us as,

01:31:38.550 --> 01:31:40.320
especially as writers of color

01:31:40.320 --> 01:31:43.890
and how we get put into categories

01:31:43.890 --> 01:31:46.560
that don't have anything
to do with beauty, joy,

01:31:46.560 --> 01:31:47.793
hope, and freedom.

01:31:49.140 --> 01:31:50.132
And I'm very suspicious of the people

01:31:50.132 --> 01:31:55.132
who wanna publish trauma
by us, they wanna publish.

01:31:55.560 --> 01:31:56.880
They'll only, you know,

01:31:56.880 --> 01:31:58.980
write to us when you
have the border story,

01:31:58.980 --> 01:32:01.380
write to us when this and
this and this and this.

01:32:01.380 --> 01:32:02.728
It's like,

01:32:02.728 --> 01:32:05.700
"Well, what if I don't have
any even signifiers in there?

01:32:05.700 --> 01:32:08.097
Would you be interested
if it was good work?"

01:32:10.867 --> 01:32:12.990
So I'm always pushing
against it and I think

01:32:12.990 --> 01:32:15.150
it doesn't mean I don't
interrogate it in my own work,

01:32:15.150 --> 01:32:18.213
it doesn't mean it's not
there, but to me it feels,

01:32:19.260 --> 01:32:21.780
You know, I grew up at a time as a writer

01:32:21.780 --> 01:32:25.560
where every poem that was
published by a black man

01:32:25.560 --> 01:32:28.350
in a certain journal, always
had to have a bullet in it

01:32:28.350 --> 01:32:30.780
in order to get published, right?

01:32:30.780 --> 01:32:32.327
And if you were Latinx,

01:32:33.478 --> 01:32:36.330
you better have (speaks in Spanish)

01:32:36.330 --> 01:32:37.590
Like it was like this very,

01:32:37.590 --> 01:32:38.670
and it was like, oh my God,

01:32:38.670 --> 01:32:42.173
this is in these big journals
that I shouldn't name,

01:32:42.173 --> 01:32:44.130
(audience laughing)

01:32:44.130 --> 01:32:45.990
and I saw that,

01:32:45.990 --> 01:32:48.510
and I remember very early on being like,

01:32:48.510 --> 01:32:52.200
I want everyone else's freedom.

01:32:52.200 --> 01:32:54.900
And guess what? They can't give it to me.

01:32:54.900 --> 01:32:56.400
I can only take it for myself.

01:32:57.660 --> 01:32:59.149
- [Audience Member] Thank
you. That was wonderful.

01:32:59.149 --> 01:33:00.116
- Thank you.

01:33:00.116 --> 01:33:03.199
(audience clapping)

01:33:04.374 --> 01:33:06.677
Thank you so much.

01:33:06.677 --> 01:33:07.510
Thank you.

01:33:07.510 --> 01:33:11.850
(audience clapping continues)

01:33:11.850 --> 01:33:15.100
(gentle digital music)

